{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nA rise of $600$ feet is required to get a railroad line over a mountain. The grade can be kept down by lengthening the track and curving it around the mountain peak. The additional length of track required to reduce the grade from $3\\%$ to $2\\%$ is approximately: \n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 10000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 20000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 30000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 12000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\text{none of these}$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"grade slope percentage rise run railroad track\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] slope_def_field  (theorem)\n    (f : k → k) (a b : k) : slope f a b = (f b - f a) / (b - a)\n    For any function $f : k \\to k$ and elements $a, b \\in k$, the slope of $f$ between $a$ and $b$ is equal to the difference quotient $\\frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}$, where $k$ is a field.\n[2] slope_def_module  (theorem)\n    (f : k → E) (a b : k) : slope f a b = (b - a)⁻¹ • (f b - f a)\n    For a function $ f : k \\to E $ and points $ a, b \\in k $, the slope of $ f $ at $ a $ and $ b $ is given by $ \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot (f(b) - f(a)) $, where $ \\cdot $ denotes scalar multiplication in the module $ E $ over the field $ k $.\n[3] slope  (definition)\n    (f : k → PE) (a b : k) : E\n    The slope of a function $ f $ at points $ a $ and $ b $ is defined as $ \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot (f(b) - f(a)) $, where $ f(b) - f(a) $ denotes vector subtraction in the affine space $ E $ and $ \\frac{1}{b - a} $ denotes scalar multiplication by the inverse of $ b - a $ in the field $ k $.\n[4] slope_fun_def  (theorem)\n    (f : k → PE) : slope f = fun a b => (b - a)⁻¹ • (f b -ᵥ f a)\n    The slope of a function $ f $ at points $ a $ and $ b $ is given by $ \\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot (f(b) - f(a)) $, where $ f(b) - f(a) $ denotes vector subtraction in the affine space $ E $ and $ \\frac{1}{b - a} $ denotes scalar multiplication by the inverse of $ b - a $ in the field $ k $.\n[5] slope_fun_def_field  (theorem)\n    (f : k → k) (a : k) : slope f a = fun b => (f b - f a) / (b - a)\n    For any function $ f : k \\to k $ and any point $ a \\in k $, where $ k $ is a field, the slope function $ \\text{slope} \\, f \\, a $ is equal to the function that maps each $ b \\in k $ to $ \\frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a} $.\n[6] slope_nonneg_iff_of_le  (theorem)\n    (hxy : x ≤ y) : 0 ≤ slope f x y ↔ f x ≤ f y\n    For any elements $x, y$ in the field $k$ with $x \\leq y$, the slope of the function $f$ at $x$ and $y$ is non-negative if and only if $f(x) \\leq f(y)$. The slope is defined as $\\frac{1}{y - x} \\cdot (f(y) - f(x))$, where $f(y) - f(x)$ denotes vector subtraction in the affine space $E$ and $\\frac{1}{y - x}$ denotes scalar multiplication by the inverse of $y - x$ in the field $k$.\n[7] slope_eq_zero_iff  (theorem)\n    {f : k → E} {a b : k} : slope f a b = 0 ↔ f a = f b\n    For any function $ f : k \\to E $ and any points $ a, b \\in k $, the slope of $ f $ between $ a $ and $ b $ is zero if and only if $ f(a) = f(b) $.\n[8] slope_pos_iff_of_le  (theorem)\n    (hxy : x ≤ y) : 0 < slope f x y ↔ f x < f y\n    For any $x$ and $y$ with $x \\leq y$, the slope of the function $f$ between $x$ and $y$ is positive if and only if $f(x) < f(y)$. The slope is defined as $\\frac{1}{y - x} \\cdot (f(y) - f(x))$, where $f(y) - f(x)$ denotes vector subtraction in the affine space and $\\frac{1}{y - x}$ denotes scalar multiplication by the inverse of $y - x$ in the field.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"railroad grade percentage formula rise run slope\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Calculating Track Grade for a Given Rise and Run\n    Grade calculations.\nI have seen this question a few times so I'll provide some math. The question is how do I figure out what grade I have specifically ...\n    https://www.facebook.com/groups/438725726326410/posts/2701226776742949/\n[2] Use This Model Railroad Track Grade Calculator\n    The amount of slope on a track grade is based on the rise (in feet) per 100 foot of track length, and is expressed as a percentage e.g. 1ft in a 100ft = 1%.\n    https://www.modelbuildings.org/track-grade-calculator/\n[3] Model train grades : Achieving the proper rise with minimal run (length)\n    Talking about the concept of track grade and how to achieve the the highest possible grade with the minimal amount of run or length.\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OXH6-1L2lPs\n[4] Slope Percentage Calculator | Convert Grade, Angle & Ratio (1 in N)\n    By definition, slope % = (rise / run) × 100. The angle obeys θ = arctan(rise/run), and the common “1 in N” ratio means rise = 1 for run = N, ...\n    https://www.pearson.com/channels/calculators/slope-percentage-calculator\n[5] Grades for Model Railroads\n    A grade that rises three feet for every 100 feet it travels would be considered a 3% grade. On a busy mainline, any extended grade over 1.5% is generally ...\n    http://www.lionel.com/articles/grades-for-model-railroads/\n</tool_response>\nNow I understand the problem. The grade is defined as (rise / run) × 100%, where \"run\" is the horizontal distance. However, for railroad tracks, the track length is the hypotenuse of the right triangle. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nFor a grade of g%, we have:\n- grade = (rise / horizontal_distance) × 100\n- So horizontal_distance = rise / (grade/100)\n\nBut the track length is the hypotenuse: track_length = √(rise² + horizontal_distance²)\n\nFor small grades, the track length ≈ horizontal_distance (since the rise is much smaller than the run).\n\nLet me verify: For 3% grade with 600 ft rise:\n- horizontal_distance = 600 / 0.03 = 20,000 ft\n- track_length = √(600² + 20000²) ≈ √(360000 + 400000000) ≈ √400360000 ≈ 20009 ft\n\nFor 2% grade with 600 ft rise:\n- horizontal_distance = 600 / 0.02 = 30,000 ft  \n- track_length = √(600² + 30000²) ≈ √(360000 + 900000000) ≈ √900360000 ≈ 30006 ft\n\nAdditional length ≈ 30006 - 20009 ≈ 9997 ft ≈ 10,000 ft\n\nSo the answer should be approximately 10,000 ft (option A).\n\nLet me formalize this in Lean 4:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nA rise of 600 feet is required to get a railroad line over a mountain. \nThe grade (expressed as a percentage) is defined as (rise / horizontal_distance) × 100.\nThe track length is the hypotenuse of the right triangle formed by the rise and horizontal distance.\nThis theorem states that the additional track length required to reduce the grade from 3% to 2% \nis approximately 10000 feet.\n-/\ntheorem railroad_grade_track_length : \n  let rise : ℝ := 600\n  let grade1 : ℝ := 3  -- initial grade in percent\n  let grade2 : ℝ := 2  -- reduced grade in percent\n  let horizontal_dist1 : ℝ := rise / (grade1 / 100)\n  let horizontal_dist2 : ℝ := rise / (grade2 / 100)\n  let track_length1 : ℝ := Real.sqrt (rise^2 + horizontal_dist1^2)\n  let track_length2 : ℝ := Real.sqrt (rise^2 + horizontal_dist2^2)\n  let additional_length : ℝ := track_length2 - track_length1\n  additional_length ≈ 10000 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: failed to synthesize instance of type class\n  HasEquiv ℝ\n\nHint: Type class instance resolution failures can be inspected with the `set_option trace.Meta.synthInstance true` command.\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"approximation real numbers absolute difference bound\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.abs_ofDigits_sub_ofDigits_le  (theorem)\n    {b : ℕ} {x y : ℕ → Fin b} {n : ℕ} (hxy : ∀ i < n, x i = y i) : |ofDigits x - ofDigits y| ≤ ((b : ℝ) ^ n)⁻¹\n    Given a base $ b $ and two sequences of digits $ x, y : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\{0, 1, \\ldots, b-1\\} $, if $ x(i) = y(i) $ for all $ i < n $, then the absolute difference between the real numbers represented by these digit sequences satisfies $ |\\text{ofDigits}(x) - \\text{ofDigits}(y)| \\leq \\frac{1}{b^n} $. This bound arises because the first differing digit occurs at position $ n $, contributing at most $ \\frac{1}{b^n} $ to the difference.\n[2] Real.exp_approx_start  (theorem)\n    (x a b : ℝ) (h : |exp x - expNear 0 x a| ≤ |x| ^ 0 / Nat.factorial 0 * b) : |exp x - a| ≤ b\n    For real numbers $x$, $a$, and $b$, if the absolute difference between $e^x$ and the zeroth-order approximation $\\text{expNear}(0, x, a)$ satisfies $|e^x - \\text{expNear}(0, x, a)| \\leq b$, then $|e^x - a| \\leq b$.\n[3] Real.exp_approx_end  (theorem)\n    (n m : ℕ) (x : ℝ) (e₁ : n + 1 = m) (h : |x| ≤ 1) : |exp x - expNear m x 0| ≤ |x| ^ m / m.factorial * ((m + 1) / m)\n    For natural numbers $n$ and $m = n+1$, and a real number $x$ with $|x| \\leq 1$, the absolute difference between the exponential function $e^x$ and its truncated Taylor series approximation $\\sum_{k=0}^{m-1} \\frac{x^k}{k!}$ is bounded by $\\frac{|x|^m}{m!} \\cdot \\frac{m+1}{m}$. This provides an explicit remainder estimate for the Taylor approximation of $e^x$ under the given constraints.\n[4] Real.exists_nat_abs_mul_sub_round_le  (theorem)\n    (ξ : ℝ) {n : ℕ} (n_pos : 0 < n) : ∃ k : ℕ, 0 < k ∧ k ≤ n ∧ |↑k * ξ - round (↑k * ξ)| ≤ 1 / (n + 1)\n    For any real number $\\xi$ and positive integer $n$, there exists a natural number $k$ satisfying $1 \\leq k \\leq n$ such that the absolute difference between $k\\xi$ and the nearest integer is at most $\\frac{1}{n + 1}$, i.e., $\\left| k\\xi - \\text{round}(k\\xi) \\right| \\leq \\frac{1}{n + 1}$.\n[5] Real.exists_rat_abs_sub_le_and_den_le  (theorem)\n    (ξ : ℝ) {n : ℕ} (n_pos : 0 < n) : ∃ q : ℚ, |ξ - q| ≤ 1 / ((n + 1) * q.den) ∧ q.den ≤ n\n    For any real number $\\xi$ and positive integer $n$, there exists a rational number $q$ with denominator $\\leq n$ such that $|\\xi - q| \\leq \\frac{1}{(n + 1) \\cdot \\text{den}(q)}$.\n</tool_response>\nLooking at the error, the issue is that `let` bindings inside the theorem type are not allowed in Lean 4.29, and `≈` requires a `HasEquiv` instance that doesn't exist for `ℝ`. I need to:\n\n1. Remove `let` bindings from the theorem type (write a pure proposition)\n2. Replace `≈` with an explicit approximation bound using absolute difference\n\nLet me rewrite this correctly:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nA rise of 600 feet is required to get a railroad line over a mountain. \nThe grade (expressed as a percentage) is defined as (rise / horizontal_distance) × 100.\nThe track length is the hypotenuse of the right triangle formed by the rise and horizontal distance.\nThis theorem states that the additional track length required to reduce the grade from 3% to 2% \nis approximately 10000 feet (within 100 feet).\n-/\ntheorem railroad_grade_track_length : \n  |(Real.sqrt (600^2 + (600 / (2 / 100))^2) - Real.sqrt (600^2 + (600 / (3 / 100))^2)) - 10000| < 100 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the additional track length required to reduce the grade from 3% to 2% given a fixed rise of 600 feet. The grade is defined as (rise / horizontal distance) * 100. Track length is the hypotenuse. Mathematically, horizontal distances are 20,000 ft (for 3%) and 30,000 ft (for 2%). Track lengths are $\\sqrt{600^2 + 20000^2} \\approx 20009$ and $\\sqrt{600^2 + 30000^2} \\approx 30006$. The difference is approximately 9997 ft, which is approximately 10,000 ft.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean statement defines `rise` as 600, grades as 3 and 2. It calculates horizontal distances as `rise / (grade / 100)`, which matches the grade definition. It calculates track lengths using `Real.sqrt (rise^2 + dist^2)`. It defines `additional_length` as the difference. The conclusion asserts `additional_length ≈ 10000`.\n3. Comparative Verification: The mathematical logic, variable definitions, and calculations in the Lean statement exactly match the natural language problem. The only discrepancy is the use of the symbol `≈`, which is not a standard defined relation for Real numbers in Lean (causing the `HasEquiv` error mentioned in the history). To be a valid Lean statement, `≈` should be replaced by a concrete inequality such as `|additional_length - 10000| < 100`, which matches the \"approximately\" condition and the previous valid iteration.\n4. Conclusion: The mathematical formalization is exact and faithful. The syntax error (`≈`) is a minor representational issue that is resolved by replacing it with the standard inequality for approximation (as implied by the instruction to fix errors).\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All mathematical components (rise, grades, formulas, result) correspond perfectly. The Lean statement correctly encodes the problem's logic. The symbol `≈` is semantically consistent with \"approximately\" but requires syntactic correction to `|... - 10000| < 100` for Lean validity.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nA rise of 600 feet is required to get a railroad line over a mountain. \nThe grade (expressed as a percentage) is defined as (rise / horizontal_distance) × 100.\nThe track length is the hypotenuse of the right triangle formed by the rise and horizontal distance.\nThis theorem states that the additional track length required to reduce the grade from 3% to 2% \nis approximately 10000 feet (within 100 feet).\n-/\ntheorem railroad_grade_track_length : \n  |(Real.sqrt (600^2 + (600 / (2 / 100))^2) - Real.sqrt (600^2 + (600 / (3 / 100))^2)) - 10000| < 100 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "A rise of $600$ feet is required to get a railroad line over a mountain. The grade can be kept down by lengthening the track and curving it around the mountain peak. The additional length of track required to reduce the grade from $3\\%$ to $2\\%$ is approximately: \n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 10000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 20000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 30000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 12000\\text{ ft.}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\text{none of these}$", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 3, "tools_used": ["leansearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_amc_aime", "question_id": "amc_aime_198205"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n/-- Suppose $p$ is an odd prime. Prove that $\\sum_{j=0}^p \\binom{p}{j}\\binom{p+j}{j} \\equiv 2^p+1 \\pmod{p^2}$.-/"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"binomial coefficient congruence modulo prime squared\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.factorization_choose_le_one  (theorem)\n    (p_large : n < p ^ 2) : (choose n k).factorization p ≤ 1\n    For a prime $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k $ such that $ n < p^2 $, the exponent of $ p $ in the prime factorization of the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ satisfies $ v_p\\left(\\binom{n}{k}\\right) \\leq 1 $.\n[2] Choose.choose_modEq_choose_mod_mul_choose_div  (theorem)\n    : choose n k ≡ choose (n % p) (k % p) * choose (n / p) (k / p) [ZMOD p]\n    For a prime number $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k $, the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ satisfies the congruence $ \\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\binom{n \\bmod p}{k \\bmod p} \\cdot \\binom{\\lfloor n/p \\rfloor}{\\lfloor k/p \\rfloor} \\pmod{p} $, where $ \\lfloor x \\rfloor $ denotes integer division (floor function).\n[3] Nat.Prime.dvd_choose_pow  (theorem)\n    (hp : Prime p) (hk : k ≠ 0) (hkp : k ≠ p ^ n) : p ∣ (p ^ n).choose k\n    For a prime number $ p $, positive integers $ n $ and $ k $ with $ 0 < k < p^n $, the prime $ p $ divides the binomial coefficient $\\binom{p^n}{k}$.\n[4] Choose.choose_modEq_choose_mod_mul_choose_div_nat  (theorem)\n    : choose n k ≡ choose (n % p) (k % p) * choose (n / p) (k / p) [MOD p]\n    For any prime number $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k $, the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ satisfies the congruence $ \\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\binom{n \\bmod p}{k \\bmod p} \\cdot \\binom{\\lfloor n/p \\rfloor}{\\lfloor k/p \\rfloor} \\pmod{p} $. This is the fundamental identity in Lucas' theorem, which decomposes binomial coefficients modulo primes using digit-wise operations in base $ p $.\n[5] Choose.choose_modEq_prod_range_choose  (theorem)\n    {a : ℕ} (ha₁ : n < p ^ a) (ha₂ : k < p ^ a) :\n  choose n k ≡ ∏ i ∈ range a, choose (n / p ^ i % p) (k / p ^ i % p) [ZMOD p]\n    For a prime $ p $ and integers $ n, k < p^a $, the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ is congruent modulo $ p $ to the product of binomial coefficients of the base-$ p $ digits of $ n $ and $ k $: $ \\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\prod_{i=0}^{a-1} \\binom{n_i}{k_i} \\mod p $, where $ n_i = \\lfloor n/p^i \\rfloor \\mod p $ and $ k_i = \\lfloor k/p^i \\rfloor \\mod p $.\n[6] Nat.Prime.emultiplicity_choose_prime_pow  (theorem)\n    {p n k : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (hkn : k ≤ p ^ n) (hk0 : k ≠ 0) :\n  emultiplicity p (choose (p ^ n) k) = ↑(n - multiplicity p k)\n    For any prime number $ p $, natural numbers $ n $ and $ k $ with $ 1 \\leq k \\leq p^n $, the exponential multiplicity of $ p $ in the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{p^n}{k} $ equals $ n - \\text{multiplicity}(p, k) $, where $ \\text{multiplicity}(p, k) $ denotes the largest power of $ p $ dividing $ k $.\n[7] Choose.lucas_theorem_nat  (theorem)\n    ∀ {n k p : ℕ} [Fact (Nat.Prime p)] {a : ℕ},\n  n < p ^ a → k < p ^ a → n.choose k ≡ ∏ i ∈ Finset.range a, (n / p ^ i % p).choose (k / p ^ i % p) [MOD p]\n    For a prime $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k, a $, if $ n < p^a $ and $ k < p^a $, then the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ is congruent modulo $ p $ to the product of the binomial coefficients of the base-$ p $ digits of $ n $ and $ k $. Specifically, $ \\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\prod_{i=0}^{a-1} \\binom{n_i}{k_i} \\pmod{p} $, where $ n_i = \\lfloor n/p^i \\rfloor \\mod p $ and $ k_i = \\lfloor k/p^i \\rfloor \\mod p $ denote the base-$ p $ digits of $ n $ and $ k $.\n[8] Choose.choose_modEq_prod_range_choose_nat  (theorem)\n    {a : ℕ} (ha₁ : n < p ^ a) (ha₂ : k < p ^ a) :\n  choose n k ≡ ∏ i ∈ range a, choose (n / p ^ i % p) (k / p ^ i % p) [MOD p]\n    For a prime $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k < p^a $, the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ satisfies the congruence $ \\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\prod_{i=0}^{a-1} \\binom{n_i}{k_i} \\pmod{p} $, where $ n_i = \\lfloor n/p^i \\rfloor \\mod p $ and $ k_i = \\lfloor k/p^i \\rfloor \\mod p $ denote the base-$ p $ digits of $ n $ and $ k $ respectively.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"sum binomial coefficients Finset.sum range\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.sum_range_choose  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : (∑ m ∈ range (n + 1), n.choose m) = 2 ^ n\n    For any natural number $n$, the sum of the binomial coefficients $\\binom{n}{k}$ for all $k$ in the range $0 \\leq k \\leq n$ is equal to $2^n$. This identity follows from the binomial theorem applied to $(1 + 1)^n$.\n[2] Nat.sum_range_add_choose  (theorem)\n    (n k : ℕ) : ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (n + 1), (i + k).choose k = (n + k + 1).choose (k + 1)\n    For any natural numbers $n$ and $k$, the sum of $\\binom{i + k}{k}$ for $i$ ranging from $0$ to $n$ is equal to $\\binom{n + k + 1}{k + 1}$.\n[3] Finset.sum_choose_succ_mul  (theorem)\n    (f : ℕ → ℕ → R) (n : ℕ) :\n  (∑ i ∈ range (n + 2), ((n + 1).choose i : R) * f i (n + 1 - i)) =\n    (∑ i ∈ range (n + 1), (n.choose i : R) * f i (n + 1 - i)) +\n      ∑ i ∈ range (n + 1), (n.choose i : R) * f (i + 1) (n - i)\n    For any function $f : \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N} \\to R$ where $R$ is a non-associative semiring and for any natural number $n$, the sum $\\sum_{i=0}^{n+1} \\binom{n+1}{i} f(i, n+1 - i)$ equals the sum $\\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{i} f(i, n+1 - i) + \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{i} f(i+1, n - i)$.\n[4] Finset.sum_choose_succ_nsmul  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] (f : ℕ → ℕ → M) (n : ℕ),\n  ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (n + 2), (n + 1).choose i • f i (n + 1 - i) =\n    ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (n + 1), n.choose i • f i (n + 1 - i) +\n      ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (n + 1), n.choose i • f (i + 1) (n - i)\n    For any additive commutative monoid $M$ and function $f : \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N} \\to M$, the identity $$\\sum_{i=0}^{n+1} \\binom{n+1}{i} \\cdot f(i, n+1 - i) = \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{i} \\cdot f(i, n+1 - i) + \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{i} \\cdot f(i+1, n - i)$$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, where $\\cdot$ denotes natural number scalar multiplication in $M$.\n[5] Nat.sum_range_choose_halfway  (theorem)\n    (m : ℕ) : (∑ i ∈ range (m + 1), (2 * m + 1).choose i) = 4 ^ m\n    For any natural number $m$, the sum of the binomial coefficients $\\binom{2m + 1}{i}$ for $i$ ranging from $0$ to $m$ is equal to $4^m$.\n[6] Nat.sum_Icc_choose  (theorem)\n    (n k : ℕ) : ∑ m ∈ Icc k n, m.choose k = (n + 1).choose (k + 1)\n    For natural numbers $n$ and $k$, the sum of $\\binom{m}{k}$ for all $m$ in the closed interval $[k, n]$ is equal to $\\binom{n + 1}{k + 1}$.\n[7] Finset.sum_powerset_apply_card  (theorem)\n    {α β : Type*} [AddCommMonoid α] (f : ℕ → α) {x : Finset β} :\n  ∑ m ∈ x.powerset, f #m = ∑ m ∈ range (#x + 1), (#x).choose m • f m\n    For any additive commutative monoid $\\alpha$, function $f : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\alpha$, and finite set $x$ of type $\\beta$, the sum of $f$ evaluated at the cardinality of each subset of $x$ equals the sum over $m$ from $0$ to $|x|$ of $\\binom{|x|}{m} \\cdot f(m)$. Formally, $\\sum_{m \\in \\mathcal{P}(x)} f(|m|) = \\sum_{m=0}^{|x|} \\binom{|x|}{m} \\cdot f(m)$.\n[8] Int.alternating_sum_range_choose_eq_choose  (theorem)\n    {n m : ℕ} : (∑ k ∈ range (m + 1), ((-1) ^ k * (n + 1).choose k : ℤ)) = (-1) ^ m * n.choose m\n    For natural numbers $n$ and $m$, the alternating sum $\\sum_{k=0}^{m} (-1)^k \\binom{n+1}{k}$ equals $(-1)^m \\binom{n}{m}$. This identity relates binomial coefficients with alternating signs over a finite range to a single binomial coefficient scaled by a power of $-1$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"congruence modulo ZMod Int.ModEq\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Int.ModEq  (definition)\n    (n a b : ℤ)\n    Integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if their remainders upon division by $n$ are equal, i.e., $a \\bmod n = b \\bmod n$.\n[2] Int.ModEq.eq  (theorem)\n    : a ≡ b [ZMOD n] → a % n = b % n\n    If integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$, then their remainders upon division by $n$ are equal, i.e., $a \\bmod n = b \\bmod n$.\n[3] Int.ModEq.of_dvd  (theorem)\n    (d : m ∣ n) (h : a ≡ b [ZMOD n]) : a ≡ b [ZMOD m]\n    If $ m $ divides $ n $ and $ a \\equiv b \\pmod{n} $, then $ a \\equiv b \\pmod{m} $.\n[4] Int.modEq_iff_add_fac  (theorem)\n    {a b n : ℤ} : a ≡ b [ZMOD n] ↔ ∃ t, b = a + n * t\n    For integers $a$, $b$, and $n$, $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$ if and only if there exists an integer $t$ such that $b = a + n \\cdot t$.\n[5] AddCommGroup.modEq_iff_int_modEq  (theorem)\n    ∀ {a b z : ℤ}, a ≡ b [PMOD z] ↔ a ≡ b [ZMOD z]\n    For all integers $a$, $b$, and $z$, $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $z$ in the natural number sense if and only if they are congruent modulo $z$ in the integer sense, i.e., $a \\equiv b \\pmod{z}$ holds equivalently under both interpretations.\n[6] Int.ModEq.rfl  (theorem)\n    : a ≡ a [ZMOD n]\n    For any integer $a$ and positive integer $n$, $a$ is congruent to itself modulo $n$, i.e., $a \\equiv a \\pmod{n}$.\n[7] Int.modEq_iff_dvd  (theorem)\n    : a ≡ b [ZMOD n] ↔ n ∣ b - a\n    For integers $a$, $b$, and $n$, $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $b - a$.\n[8] ZMod.natCast_eq_natCast_iff  (theorem)\n    (a b c : ℕ) : (a : ZMod c) = (b : ZMod c) ↔ a ≡ b [MOD c]\n    For natural numbers $a$, $b$, and $c$, the equality of $a$ and $b$ in the ring $\\mathbb{Z}/c\\mathbb{Z}$ holds if and only if $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $c$, i.e., $a \\equiv b \\pmod{c}$.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize the statement. Let me create the Lean 4 theorem:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Nat\n\n/-- For an odd prime p, the sum of binomial coefficients ∑_{j=0}^p (p choose j) * (p+j choose j) \n    is congruent to 2^p + 1 modulo p^2. -/\ntheorem binomial_sum_congr_mod_p_squared (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (hp_odd : Odd p) :\n  (∑ j ∈ Finset.range (p + 1), (p.choose j) * (p + j).choose j) ≡ 2 ^ p + 1 [MOD p ^ 2] := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem requires proving that for an odd prime p, the sum ∑_{j=0}^p (p choose j)(p+j choose j) ≡ 2^p + 1 mod p². Key components: p is an odd prime, sum from j=0 to p, binomial coefficients (p choose j) and (p+j choose j), congruence modulo p².\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem parameters (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (hp_odd : Odd p) correctly capture p being an odd prime. The sum ∑ j ∈ Finset.range (p + 1), (p.choose j) * (p + j).choose j matches the math sum (Finset.range (p+1) includes 0 to p). The conclusion ≡ 2^p + 1 [MOD p^2] matches the congruence modulo p².\n3. Comparative Verification: All components align: p is an odd prime (hp and hp_odd), sum bounds (Finset.range (p+1)), binomial coefficients (p.choose j and (p+j).choose j), modulus (p^2), and congruence structure. No missing elements or semantic drift.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement precisely mirrors the mathematical problem's structure and constraints.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The Lean code correctly formalizes the problem's variables, constraints, sum expression, and congruence requirement.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Nat\n\n/-- For an odd prime p, the sum of binomial coefficients ∑_{j=0}^p (p choose j) * (p+j choose j) \n    is congruent to 2^p + 1 modulo p^2. -/\ntheorem binomial_sum_congr_mod_p_squared (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (hp_odd : Odd p) :\n  (∑ j ∈ Finset.range (p + 1), (p.choose j) * (p + j).choose j) ≡ 2 ^ p + 1 [MOD p ^ 2] := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "/-- Suppose $p$ is an odd prime. Prove that $\\sum_{j=0}^p \\binom{p}{j}\\binom{p+j}{j} \\equiv 2^p+1 \\pmod{p^2}$.-/", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 3, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "putnam_v4", "question_id": "564"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nIf $\\sigma, \\tau$ are two permutations that disturb no common element and $\\sigma \\tau = e$, prove that $\\sigma = \\tau = e$."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"permutation support disjoint\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint.disjoint_support  (theorem)\n    (h : Disjoint f g) : _root_.Disjoint f.support g.support\n    If two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are disjoint (i.e., every element $ x \\in \\alpha $ is fixed by at least one of them), then their supports (the finite sets of elements not fixed by each permutation) are disjoint sets.\n[2] Equiv.Perm.disjoint_iff_disjoint_support  (theorem)\n    : Disjoint f g ↔ _root_.Disjoint f.support g.support\n    Two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are disjoint (i.e., every element is fixed by at least one of them) if and only if their supports (the sets of elements they move) are disjoint as finite sets.\n[3] Equiv.Perm.ofSubtype_support_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {σ : Perm α} (x : Perm (Function.fixedPoints σ)) : _root_.Disjoint x.ofSubtype.support σ.support\n    For any permutation $\\sigma$ on a type $\\alpha$ and any permutation $x$ on the fixed points of $\\sigma$, the support of the extended permutation $x$ (acting on $\\alpha$) is disjoint from the support of $\\sigma$. This means that no element is moved by both permutations, as $x$ only acts nontrivially on elements fixed by $\\sigma$, while $\\sigma$ only acts nontrivially on elements not fixed by it.\n[4] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint.mem_imp  (theorem)\n    (h : Disjoint f g) {x : α} (hx : x ∈ f.support) : x ∉ g.support\n    If permutations $ f $ and $ g $ are disjoint, then their supports are disjoint sets; that is, for any element $ x $, if $ x $ is in the support of $ f $, then $ x $ is not in the support of $ g $.\n[5] Equiv.Perm.support_noncommProd  (theorem)\n    {ι : Type*} {k : ι → Perm α} {s : Finset ι} (hs : Set.Pairwise s fun i j ↦ Disjoint (k i) (k j)) :\n  (s.noncommProd k (hs.imp (fun _ _ ↦ Perm.Disjoint.commute))).support = s.biUnion fun i ↦ (k i).support\n    Given a finite set $ s $ of indices and a family of permutations $ k_i $ on a set $ \\alpha $ that are pairwise disjoint (i.e., for all distinct $ i,j \\in s $, $ k_i $ and $ k_j $ are disjoint permutations), the support of the noncommutative product $ \\prod_{i \\in s} k_i $ is equal to the union of the supports of each individual permutation $ k_i $. In symbols, $ \\operatorname{supp}\\left(\\prod_{i \\in s} k_i\\right) = \\bigcup_{i \\in s} \\operatorname{supp}(k_i) $.\n[6] Equiv.Perm.disjoint_of_disjoint_support  (theorem)\n    {H K : Subgroup (Perm α)} (h : ∀ a ∈ H, ∀ b ∈ K, _root_.Disjoint a.support b.support) : _root_.Disjoint H K\n    If every permutation in subgroup $H$ has support disjoint from the support of every permutation in subgroup $K$, then $H$ and $K$ are disjoint subgroups (i.e., their intersection contains only the identity permutation).\n[7] Equiv.Perm.support_le_prod_of_mem  (theorem)\n    {l : List (Perm α)} (h : f ∈ l) (hl : l.Pairwise Disjoint) : f.support ≤ l.prod.support\n    If $ f $ is a permutation in a finite list $ l $ of pairwise disjoint permutations, then the support of $ f $ is a subset of the support of the product of all permutations in $ l $, i.e., $ \\mathrm{supp}(f) \\subseteq \\mathrm{supp}(\\prod_{g \\in l} g) $.\n[8] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint  (definition)\n    (f g : Perm α)\n    Two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are called disjoint if every element $ x \\in \\alpha $ is fixed by at least one of them, i.e., for all $ x $, either $ f(x) = x $ or $ g(x) = x $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Equiv.Perm.support\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Equiv.Perm.support  (definition)\n    (f : Perm α) : Finset α\n    The support of a permutation $ f $ is the finite set of all elements $ x $ in $ \\alpha $ such that $ f(x) \\neq x $.\n[2] Equiv.Perm.support_eq_empty_iff  (theorem)\n    {σ : Perm α} : σ.support = ∅ ↔ σ = 1\n    For any permutation $\\sigma$ on a type $\\alpha$, the support of $\\sigma$ is equal to the empty set if and only if $\\sigma$ is the identity permutation.\n[3] Equiv.Perm.set_support_apply_mem  (theorem)\n    {p : Perm α} {a : α} : p a ∈ {x | p x ≠ x} ↔ a ∈ {x | p x ≠ x}\n    For any permutation $ p $ of a type $ \\alpha $ and any element $ a \\in \\alpha $, the image $ p(a) $ lies in the support of $ p $ if and only if $ a $ lies in the support of $ p $. The support of a permutation is the set $ \\{x \\in \\alpha \\mid p(x) \\neq x\\} $.\n[4] Equiv.Perm.mem_support  (theorem)\n    {x : α} : x ∈ f.support ↔ f x ≠ x\n    An element $ x $ is in the support of a permutation $ f $ if and only if $ f(x) eq x $.\n[5] Equiv.Perm.support_congr  (theorem)\n    (h : f.support ⊆ g.support) (h' : ∀ x ∈ g.support, f x = g x) : f = g\n    Let $ f $ and $ g $ be permutations of a type $ \\alpha $. If the support of $ f $ is a subset of the support of $ g $ and for every element $ x $ in the support of $ g $, $ f(x) = g(x) $, then $ f = g $. The support of a permutation is the finite set of elements $ x $ where $ f(x) \\neq x $.\n</tool_response>\nBased on the search results, I can now formalize this theorem. The key concepts are:\n- \"disturb no common element\" means the permutations are disjoint (`Disjoint σ τ`)\n- The identity permutation is denoted as `1` in Mathlib\n- The support of a permutation is empty iff it equals the identity\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Equiv.Perm\n\n/-- If two permutations disturb no common element and their product is the identity,\n    then both permutations are the identity. -/\ntheorem perm_disjoint_prod_eq_one {α : Type*} (σ τ : Perm α) \n  (h_disjoint : Disjoint σ τ) (h_prod : σ * τ = 1) : σ = 1 ∧ τ = 1 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: Function expected at\n  Perm\nbut this term has type\n  ?m.1\n\nNote: Expected a function because this term is being applied to the argument\n  α\n\nHint: The identifier `Perm` is unknown, and Lean's `autoImplicit` option causes an unknown identifier to be treated as an implicitly bound variable with an unknown type. However, the unknown type cannot be a function, and a function is what Lean expects here. This is often the result of a typo or a missing `import` or `open` statement.; Function expected at\n  Perm\nbut this term has type\n  ?m.1\n\nNote: Expected a function because this term is being applied to the argument\n  α\n\nHint: The identifier `Perm` is unknown, and Lean's `autoImplicit` option causes an unknown identifier to be treated as an implicitly bound variable with an unknown type. However, the unknown type cannot be a function, and a function is what Lean expects here. This is often the result of a typo or a missing `import` or `open` statement.\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"permutations disjoint support Mathlib Equiv.Perm\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Equiv.Perm.disjoint_iff_disjoint_support  (theorem)\n    : Disjoint f g ↔ _root_.Disjoint f.support g.support\n    Two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are disjoint (i.e., every element is fixed by at least one of them) if and only if their supports (the sets of elements they move) are disjoint as finite sets.\n[2] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint.disjoint_support  (theorem)\n    (h : Disjoint f g) : _root_.Disjoint f.support g.support\n    If two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are disjoint (i.e., every element $ x \\in \\alpha $ is fixed by at least one of them), then their supports (the finite sets of elements not fixed by each permutation) are disjoint sets.\n[3] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint.mem_imp  (theorem)\n    (h : Disjoint f g) {x : α} (hx : x ∈ f.support) : x ∉ g.support\n    If permutations $ f $ and $ g $ are disjoint, then their supports are disjoint sets; that is, for any element $ x $, if $ x $ is in the support of $ f $, then $ x $ is not in the support of $ g $.\n[4] Equiv.Perm.ofSubtype_support_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {σ : Perm α} (x : Perm (Function.fixedPoints σ)) : _root_.Disjoint x.ofSubtype.support σ.support\n    For any permutation $\\sigma$ on a type $\\alpha$ and any permutation $x$ on the fixed points of $\\sigma$, the support of the extended permutation $x$ (acting on $\\alpha$) is disjoint from the support of $\\sigma$. This means that no element is moved by both permutations, as $x$ only acts nontrivially on elements fixed by $\\sigma$, while $\\sigma$ only acts nontrivially on elements not fixed by it.\n[5] Equiv.Perm.disjoint_iff_eq_or_eq  (theorem)\n    : Disjoint f g ↔ ∀ x : α, f x = x ∨ g x = x\n    Two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are disjoint if and only if for every element $ x \\in \\alpha $, either $ f(x) = x $ or $ g(x) = x $.\n[6] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint  (definition)\n    (f g : Perm α)\n    Two permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $ are called disjoint if every element $ x \\in \\alpha $ is fixed by at least one of them, i.e., for all $ x $, either $ f(x) = x $ or $ g(x) = x $.\n[7] Equiv.Perm.disjoint_of_disjoint_support  (theorem)\n    {H K : Subgroup (Perm α)} (h : ∀ a ∈ H, ∀ b ∈ K, _root_.Disjoint a.support b.support) : _root_.Disjoint H K\n    If every permutation in subgroup $H$ has support disjoint from the support of every permutation in subgroup $K$, then $H$ and $K$ are disjoint subgroups (i.e., their intersection contains only the identity permutation).\n[8] Equiv.Perm.Disjoint.support_mul  (theorem)\n    (h : Disjoint f g) : (f * g).support = f.support ∪ g.support\n    If $ f $ and $ g $ are disjoint permutations on a set $ \\alpha $, then the support of their product $ f \\cdot g $ (defined as the composition of $ g $ followed by $ f $) is equal to the union of the supports of $ f $ and $ g $. That is, $ (f \\cdot g).\\text{support} = f.\\text{support} \\cup g.\\text{support} $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Perm type alias Equiv.Perm Mathlib import\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Equiv.Perm  (abbrev)\n    (α : Sort*)\n    The type of permutations on α, i.e., bijective functions from α to α.\n[2] Equiv.Perm.ext_iff  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Sort u} {σ τ : Equiv.Perm α}, σ = τ ↔ ∀ (x : α), σ x = τ x\n    For any type $\\alpha$, two permutations $\\sigma$ and $\\tau$ on $\\alpha$ satisfy $\\sigma = \\tau$ if and only if $\\sigma(x) = \\tau(x)$ for all $x \\in \\alpha$.\n[3] Equiv.Perm.ext  (theorem)\n    {σ τ : Equiv.Perm α} (H : ∀ x, σ x = τ x) : σ = τ\n    If two permutations $\\sigma$ and $\\tau$ on a type $\\alpha$ satisfy $\\sigma(x) = \\tau(x)$ for all $x \\in \\alpha$, then $\\sigma = \\tau$.\n[4] fintypePerm  (definition)\n    [Fintype α] : Fintype (Perm α)\n    Given a finite type $\\alpha$, the set of all permutations on $\\alpha$ forms a finite type. This is constructed by generating all permutations of the universal finite set $\\text{Finset.univ}$ of $\\alpha$ using $\\text{permsOfFinset}$, ensuring the result is a finite collection of permutations.\n[5] Equiv.Perm.congr_fun  (theorem)\n    {f g : Equiv.Perm α} (h : f = g) (x : α) : f x = g x\n    For any permutations $ f $ and $ g $ on a set $ \\alpha $, if $ f = g $, then for every $ x \\in \\alpha $, $ f(x) = g(x) $.\n</tool_response>\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Equiv.Perm\n\n/-- If two permutations disturb no common element and their product is the identity,\n    then both permutations are the identity. -/\ntheorem perm_disjoint_prod_eq_one {α : Type*} (σ τ : Equiv.Perm α) \n  (h_disjoint : Disjoint σ τ) (h_prod : σ * τ = 1) : σ = 1 ∧ τ = 1 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: \n   - Variables: $\\sigma, \\tau$ (permutations on some set).\n   - Constraints: \n     1. $\\sigma, \\tau$ disturb no common element (disjoint supports).\n     2. $\\sigma \\tau = e$ (product is identity).\n   - Conclusion: $\\sigma = e$ and $\\tau = e$.\n   \n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored):\n   - Variables: `{α : Type*}`, `σ τ : Perm α`.\n   - Hypotheses: \n     1. `h_disjoint : Disjoint σ τ` (matches \"disturb no common element\" via support disjointness).\n     2. `h_prod : σ * τ = 1` (matches product is identity).\n   - Conclusion: `σ = 1 ∧ τ = 1` (matches $\\sigma = \\tau = e$).\n   - Note: `Perm` is an abbreviation for `Equiv.Perm`. `1` is the identity permutation. `*` is composition.\n\n3. Comparative Verification:\n   - \"Disturb no common element\" corresponds precisely to `Disjoint σ τ` in Mathlib (defined as disjoint supports).\n   - \"$\\sigma \\tau = e$\" corresponds to `σ * τ = 1`.\n   - \"$\\sigma = \\tau = e$\" corresponds to `σ = 1 ∧ τ = 1`.\n   - Variable domains (permutations on a type `α`) match.\n   - The logical structure (implication from hypotheses to conjunction conclusion) matches.\n   - Minor syntax note: `Perm` typically requires `open Equiv` to be in scope as a standalone identifier, whereas `open Equiv.Perm` opens the namespace for lemmas. However, semantically `Perm α` clearly refers to `Equiv.Perm α`.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a mathematically exact formalization of the natural language problem.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All mathematical components (variables, constraints, conclusion) align perfectly. The use of `Disjoint` for permutations correctly captures the \"no common disturbed element\" condition. The group operation and identity element are correctly represented.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Equiv.Perm\n\n/-- If two permutations disturb no common element and their product is the identity,\n    then both permutations are the identity. -/\ntheorem perm_disjoint_prod_eq_one {α : Type*} (σ τ : Equiv.Perm α) \n  (h_disjoint : Disjoint σ τ) (h_prod : σ * τ = 1) : σ = 1 ∧ τ = 1 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "If $\\sigma, \\tau$ are two permutations that disturb no common element and $\\sigma \\tau = e$, prove that $\\sigma = \\tau = e$.", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 4, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "proofnet_test", "question_id": "51"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nThree distinct integers are selected at random between $1$ and $2016$, inclusive. Which of the following is a correct statement about the probability $p$ that the product of the three integers is odd?\n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ p\\dfrac{1}{3}$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Nat.choose binomial coefficient probability odd product\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.choose_eq_factorial_div_factorial  (theorem)\n    {n k : ℕ} (hk : k ≤ n) : choose n k = n ! / (k ! * (n - k)!)\n    For natural numbers $n$ and $k$ with $k \\leq n$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{k}$ satisfies $\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k! \\cdot (n - k)!}$.\n[2] Nat.choose_eq_choose_pred_add  (theorem)\n    {n k : ℕ} (hn : 0 < n) (hk : 0 < k) : choose n k = choose (n - 1) (k - 1) + choose (n - 1) k\n    For any positive integers $n$ and $k$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{k}$ satisfies the identity $\\binom{n}{k} = \\binom{n-1}{k-1} + \\binom{n-1}{k}$.\n[3] Nat.choose_symm_half  (theorem)\n    (m : ℕ) : choose (2 * m + 1) (m + 1) = choose (2 * m + 1) m\n    For any natural number $m$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{2m + 1}{m + 1}$ is equal to $\\binom{2m + 1}{m}$.\n[4] Nat.add_choose  (theorem)\n    (i j : ℕ) : (i + j).choose j = (i + j)! / (i ! * j !)\n    For any natural numbers $i$ and $j$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{i + j}{j}$ is equal to the quotient of the factorial of $i + j$ divided by the product of the factorials of $i$ and $j$, i.e., $\\binom{i + j}{j} = \\frac{(i + j)!}{i! \\, j!}$.\n[5] Nat.fast_choose  (definition)\n    n k\n    The function maps natural numbers $ n $ and $ k $ to the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $, defined as the descending factorial $ n \\times (n - 1) \\times \\cdots \\times (n - k + 1) $ divided by $ k! $.\n[6] Nat.choose_eq_asc_factorial_div_factorial  (theorem)\n    (n k : ℕ) : (n + k).choose k = (n + 1).ascFactorial k / k !\n    For all natural numbers $n$ and $k$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n + k}{k}$ is equal to the product $(n + 1)(n + 2)\\cdots(n + k)$ divided by $k!$.\n[7] Nat.choose  (definition)\n    : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ\n    The function $\\binom{n}{k}$ defined recursively by three cases: $\\binom{n}{0} = 1$ for any $n$, $\\binom{0}{k} = 0$ for $k > 0$, and $\\binom{n+1}{k+1} = \\binom{n}{k} + \\binom{n}{k+1}$ for positive $n, k$. This defines the number of ways to choose $k$ elements from a set of $n$ elements.\n[8] Nat.choose_mul  (theorem)\n    {n k s : ℕ} (hsk : s ≤ k) : n.choose k * k.choose s = n.choose s * (n - s).choose (k - s)\n    For natural numbers $n$, $k$, and $s$ with $s \\leq k$, the product of the binomial coefficients $\\binom{n}{k}$ and $\\binom{k}{s}$ equals the product of $\\binom{n}{s}$ and $\\binom{n - s}{k - s}$. That is, $\\binom{n}{k} \\cdot \\binom{k}{s} = \\binom{n}{s} \\cdot \\binom{n - s}{k - s}$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset card range interval closed probability rational\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finset.card_range  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : #(range n) = n\n    For any natural number $n$, the cardinality of the finite set containing all natural numbers from $0$ up to $n - 1$ is equal to $n$, i.e., $\\#\\text{range}(n) = n$.\n[2] Fin.card_Icc  (theorem)\n    : #(Icc a b) = b + 1 - a\n    For elements $a, b$ in $\\mathrm{Fin}\\, n$ with $a \\leq b$, the cardinality of the closed interval $[a, b]$ is equal to $b + 1 - a$.\n[3] Finset.card_Ico_finset  (theorem)\n    (h : s ⊆ t) : (Ico s t).card = 2 ^ (t.card - s.card) - 1\n    For finite sets $s$ and $t$ with $s \\subseteq t$, the cardinality of the interval $[s, t)$ (defined as all finite sets $u$ such that $s \\subseteq u \\subset t$) is $2^{\\lvert t \\rvert - \\lvert s \\rvert} - 1$.\n[4] Finset.card_Icc_finset  (theorem)\n    (h : s ⊆ t) : (Icc s t).card = 2 ^ (t.card - s.card)\n    For any finite sets $ s $ and $ t $ such that $ s \\subseteq t $, the cardinality of the interval $[s, t]$ (i.e., the set of all finite sets $ u $ satisfying $ s \\subseteq u \\subseteq t $) is equal to $ 2^{|t| - |s|} $.\n[5] Nat.card_Icc  (theorem)\n    : #(Icc a b) = b + 1 - a\n    For any natural numbers $a$ and $b$ with $a \\leq b$, the cardinality of the closed interval $[a, b]$ is given by $b + 1 - a$.\n[6] Finset.range_eq_Ico  (theorem)\n    : range = Ico 0\n    For any natural number $n$, the finite set of natural numbers from $0$ to $n - 1$ is equal to the closed interval $[0, n - 1]$, i.e., $\\text{Finset.range}(n) = \\text{Finset.Ico}(0, n)$. This equality holds because both constructions generate the same set of integers between $0$ and $n - 1$ under the standard ordering of natural numbers.\n[7] PNat.card_Icc  (theorem)\n    : #(Icc a b) = b + 1 - a\n    The cardinality of the closed interval $[a, b]$ in the positive natural numbers is equal to $b + 1 - a$.\n[8] Fintype.card_Icc  (theorem)\n    (a b : α) [Fintype (Set.Icc a b)] : Fintype.card (Set.Icc a b) = #(Icc a b)\n    For a type $\\alpha$ equipped with a locally finite order, the cardinality of the finite set corresponding to the closed interval $[a, b]$ is equal to the number of elements in the interval $[a, b]$, i.e., $\\#(\\text{Icc } a b) = \\#\\{z \\mid a \\leq z \\leq b\\}$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset.powersetCard filter odd probability\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finset.powersetCard_eq_filter  (theorem)\n    {n} {s : Finset α} : powersetCard n s = (powerset s).filter fun x => x.card = n\n    For a finite set $ s $ and a natural number $ n $, the set of all subsets of $ s $ with exactly $ n $ elements is equal to the set obtained by filtering the powerset of $ s $ to include only those subsets with cardinality $ n $. Formally, $ \\mathcal{P}_n(s) = \\{ t \\subseteq s \\mid |t| = n \\} $, where $ \\mathcal{P}_n(s) $ denotes the fixed-cardinality powerset.\n[2] Finset.card_powerset  (theorem)\n    (s : Finset α) : card (powerset s) = 2 ^ card s\n    The cardinality of the powerset of a finite set $ s $ is $ 2^{|s|} $, where $ |s| $ denotes the number of elements in $ s $.\n[3] Fintype.card_set  (theorem)\n    [Fintype α] : Fintype.card (Set α) = 2 ^ Fintype.card α\n    For any finite type $\\alpha$, the cardinality of its powerset is $2^{\\# \\alpha}$, i.e., $\\# \\mathcal{P}(\\alpha) = 2^{\\# \\alpha}$.\n[4] Finset.card_Iic_finset  (theorem)\n    : (Iic s).card = 2 ^ s.card\n    The cardinality of the power set of a finite set $ s $ is $ 2^{|s|} $, where $ |s| $ denotes the number of elements in $ s $.\n[5] Finset.filter_subset_univ  (theorem)\n    [DecidableEq α] (s : Finset α) : ({t | t ⊆ s} : Finset _) = powerset s\n    For a finite set $ s $ over a type $ \\alpha $ with decidable equality, the set of all subsets $ t $ of $ s $ (i.e., $ \\{t \\mid t \\subseteq s\\} $) is equal to the powerset of $ s $.\n[6] Set.ncard_powerset  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} (s : Set α), autoParam s.Finite Set.ncard_powerset._auto_1 → (𝒫 s).ncard = 2 ^ s.ncard\n    For any finite set $ S $, the cardinality of its power set $ \\mathcal{P}(S) $ is equal to $ 2^{|S|} $.\n[7] Fintype.card_finset  (theorem)\n    [Fintype α] : Fintype.card (Finset α) = 2 ^ Fintype.card α\n    For any finite type $\\alpha$, the cardinality of the set of all finite subsets of $\\alpha$ is equal to $2$ raised to the power of the cardinality of $\\alpha$, i.e., $\\# (\\mathrm{Finset}\\ \\alpha) = 2^{\\# \\alpha}$.\n[8] Set.sized_powersetCard  (theorem)\n    (s : Finset α) (r : ℕ) : (powersetCard r s : Set (Finset α)).Sized r\n    For any finite set $ s $ and natural number $ r $, the fixed-cardinality powerset of $ s $ with parameter $ r $ consists precisely of all subsets of $ s $ with exactly $ r $ elements.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize this problem. Let me analyze:\n\n1. We're selecting 3 distinct integers from {1, 2, ..., 2016}\n2. The product is odd if and only if all three integers are odd\n3. In the range 1 to 2016, there are 1008 odd numbers (1, 3, 5, ..., 2015)\n4. Total ways to choose 3 distinct integers: C(2016, 3)\n5. Favorable ways (all odd): C(1008, 3)\n6. Probability p = C(1008, 3) / C(2016, 3)\n\nThe problem asks which statement about p is correct, with option (A) being p < 1/3 (based on the incomplete statement \"p\\dfrac{1}{3}\" which likely means \"p < 1/3\").\n\nLet me formalize this:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Finset Nat\n\n/-- \nThree distinct integers are selected at random from {1, 2, ..., 2016}.\nThe probability p that their product is odd equals C(1008, 3) / C(2016, 3),\nwhich is less than 1/8.\n-/\ntheorem probability_product_odd : \n  let S := Icc 1 2016\n  let odd_subset := filter (fun x => x % 2 = 1) S\n  let total_triples := powersetCard 3 S\n  let favorable_triples := powersetCard 3 odd_subset\n  let p : ℚ := (favorable_triples.card : ℚ) / (total_triples.card : ℚ)\n  p < 1/8 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks which statement about probability p (that the product of three distinct randomly selected integers from 1-2016 is odd) is correct. Option (A) claims p < 1/3. The key components are: selection of 3 distinct integers from 1-2016, product oddness condition, probability calculation, and comparison to 1/3.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean code defines p as C(1008,3)/C(2016,3) and asserts p < 1/8. It correctly models the selection process (using subsets of size 3 from 1-2016), identifies odd numbers (1008 elements), and calculates the probability ratio. However, the theorem's conclusion is p < 1/8 instead of the problem's option (A) p < 1/3.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean code's conclusion (p < 1/8) does not match the problem's option (A) (p < 1/3). While p < 1/8 implies p < 1/3, the formalization addresses a different inequality than what the problem explicitly asks to validate. The problem requires evaluating the correctness of option (A), but the Lean code proves a stronger, unrelated bound.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement does not formalize the problem's question because it replaces the target inequality (1/3) with a different one (1/8).\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: Mismatch in the inequality being validated (1/8 vs 1/3). The problem asks to verify option (A)'s claim (p < 1/3), but the Lean code proves p < 1/8, which is a different mathematical statement. This breaks the required correspondence.\n\nResult: Incorrect\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Three distinct integers are selected at random between 1 and 2016 inclusive probability product is odd AMC\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] 2016 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 12 - AoPS Wiki\n    Three distinct integers are selected at random between $1$ and $2016$ , inclusive. For the product to be odd, all three factors have to be odd. The probability ...\n    https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2016_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12?srsltid=AfmBOorHYecms5qOJmuKvhqztTgapfWsZI8fViHw23dp2eUsKVUbiZnm\n[2] 2016 AMC 10A Real Questions and Analysis\n    Three distinct integers are selected at random between 1 and 2016, inclusive. What is the probability that the product of the three integers is ...\n    https://www.thethinkacademy.com/blog/2016-amc-10a-real-questions-and-analysis/\n[3] 2016 AMC 10A Solutions\n    Three distinct integers are selected at random between 1 1 1 and 2016 , The product is odd exactly when all three selected integers are odd. Because the ...\n    https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2016A/solutions\n[4] 2016 AMC 10A Problem Set | PDF | Mathematics\n    12 Three distinct integers are selected at random between 1 and 2016, inclusive. ... product of the three integers is odd? 1 1 1 1 1 1 (A) p < (B) p = (C) ...\n    https://www.scribd.com/document/736004291/2016-amc10a\n[5] 2020 AMC 10A - Problems & Solutions\n    Browse all 25 problems, answers, and detailed step-by-step solutions from the 2020 AMC 10A exam. Great practice for AMC 10, AMC 12, AIME, and other math ...\n    https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2020/part-a\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset.Icc Nat.choose probability rational division\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.choose_eq_factorial_div_factorial  (theorem)\n    {n k : ℕ} (hk : k ≤ n) : choose n k = n ! / (k ! * (n - k)!)\n    For natural numbers $n$ and $k$ with $k \\leq n$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{k}$ satisfies $\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k! \\cdot (n - k)!}$.\n[2] Nat.sum_Icc_choose  (theorem)\n    (n k : ℕ) : ∑ m ∈ Icc k n, m.choose k = (n + 1).choose (k + 1)\n    For natural numbers $n$ and $k$, the sum of $\\binom{m}{k}$ for all $m$ in the closed interval $[k, n]$ is equal to $\\binom{n + 1}{k + 1}$.\n[3] Nat.cast_choose  (theorem)\n    {a b : ℕ} (h : a ≤ b) : (b.choose a : K) = b ! / (a ! * (b - a)!)\n    For natural numbers $a$ and $b$ with $a \\leq b$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{b}{a}$ cast into a division semiring $K$ with characteristic zero is equal to the factorial of $b$ divided by the product of the factorials of $a$ and $b - a$, i.e., $\\binom{b}{a} = \\frac{b!}{a! \\cdot (b - a)!}$.\n[4] Finset.Icc  (definition)\n    (a b : α) : Finset α\n    For a type $\\alpha$ equipped with a locally finite order, this function constructs the finite set of elements $z$ satisfying $a \\le z \\le b$, corresponding to the closed interval $[a, b]$ in $\\alpha$.\n[5] Nat.cast_choose_two  (theorem)\n    (a : ℕ) : (a.choose 2 : K) = a * (a - 1) / 2\n    For any natural number $a$ and division ring $K$, the binomial coefficient $\\binom{a}{2}$ when cast to $K$ is equal to $\\frac{a(a - 1)}{2}$.\n</tool_response>\nBased on the search results, I can see this is from 2016 AMC 10A Problem 12. The actual problem asks which statement about the probability is correct. According to the provided informal statement, option (A) claims p < 1/3. I need to formalize this specific claim to match the given informal statement.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Finset Nat\n\n/-- \nThree distinct integers are selected at random from {1, 2, ..., 2016}.\nThe probability p that their product is odd equals C(1008, 3) / C(2016, 3).\nThis theorem states that p < 1/3 (option A from the problem).\n-/\ntheorem probability_product_odd : \n  let S := Icc 1 2016\n  let odd_subset := filter (fun x => x % 2 = 1) S\n  let total_triples := Nat.choose S.card 3\n  let favorable_triples := Nat.choose odd_subset.card 3\n  let p : ℚ := (favorable_triples : ℚ) / (total_triples : ℚ)\n  p < 1/3 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks to evaluate the correctness of Option (A) regarding the probability $p$ that the product of three distinct integers randomly selected from $\\{1, \\dots, 2016\\}$ is odd. Based on the context from the previous consistency check, Option (A) is interpreted as the inequality $p < \\frac{1}{3}$. The mathematical components are: selection of 3 distinct integers from 2016, the condition that their product is odd (implies all three are odd), and the comparison of the resulting probability to $1/3$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean code defines `S` as the finite set of integers from 1 to 2016 (`Icc 1 2016`). It defines `odd_subset` by filtering for odd numbers. It calculates `total_triples` as $\\binom{2016}{3}$ and `favorable_triples` as $\\binom{1008}{3}$ (since there are 1008 odd numbers). It defines `p` as the rational ratio of favorable to total triples. The theorem concludes with the assertion `p < 1/3`.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement accurately reflects the problem setup. The use of `Nat.choose` correctly models selection without replacement. The logic that the product is odd if and only if all factors are odd is correctly implemented by counting triples from the `odd_subset`. The asserted inequality `p < 1/3` matches the specific claim in Option (A) identified in the analysis. The previous inconsistency (asserting $p < 1/8$ instead of the option's $p < 1/3$) is resolved here.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct and exact formalization of the mathematical problem's Option (A).\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The variable domains, selection constraints, probability calculation, and the final inequality assertion all align perfectly with the natural language statement and its interpreted Option (A).\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Finset Nat\n\n/-- \nThree distinct integers are selected at random from {1, 2, ..., 2016}.\nThe probability p that their product is odd equals C(1008, 3) / C(2016, 3).\nThis theorem states that p < 1/3 (option A from the problem).\n-/\ntheorem probability_product_odd : \n  let S := Icc 1 2016\n  let odd_subset := filter (fun x => x % 2 = 1) S\n  let total_triples := Nat.choose S.card 3\n  let favorable_triples := Nat.choose odd_subset.card 3\n  let p : ℚ := (favorable_triples : ℚ) / (total_triples : ℚ)\n  p < 1/3 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Three distinct integers are selected at random between $1$ and $2016$, inclusive. Which of the following is a correct statement about the probability $p$ that the product of the three integers is odd?\n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ p\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 5, "tools_used": ["leansearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_amc_aime", "question_id": "amc_aime_201358"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n/-- Assume that $\\lvert f(x) \\rvert \\le 1$ and $\\lvert f''(x) \\rvert \\le 1$ for all $x$ on an interval of length at least 2. Show that $\\lvert f'(x) \\rvert \\le 2$ on the interval.-/"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"derivative bounds interval second derivative\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] trapezoidal_error_le  (theorem)\n    {f : ℝ → ℝ} {a b : ℝ} (h_df : DifferentiableOn ℝ f [[a, b]])\n  (h_ddf : DifferentiableOn ℝ (derivWithin f [[a, b]]) [[a, b]])\n  (h_ddf_integrable : IntervalIntegrable (iteratedDerivWithin 2 f [[a, b]]) volume a b) {ζ : ℝ}\n  (fpp_bound : ∀ x, |iteratedDerivWithin 2 f [[a, b]] x| ≤ ζ) {N : ℕ} (N_nonzero : 0 < N) :\n  |trapezoidal_error f N a b| ≤ |b - a| ^ 3 * ζ / (12 * N ^ 2)\n    For a twice differentiable function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ on the interval $[a, b]$ with second derivative bounded by $ \\zeta $ in absolute value, the trapezoidal rule approximation error using $ N $ subintervals satisfies $ |\\text{error}| \\leq \\frac{|b - a|^3 \\zeta}{12 N^2} $. This bound requires $ f $ to be twice differentiable on $[a, b]$, the second derivative $ f'' $ to be integrable, and $ N $ to be a positive integer.\n[2] norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le_segment  (theorem)\n    {C : ℝ} (hf : DifferentiableOn ℝ f (Icc a b)) (bound : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ‖derivWithin f (Icc a b) x‖ ≤ C) :\n  ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, ‖f x - f a‖ ≤ C * (x - a)\n    If a function $ f $ is differentiable on the closed interval $[a, b]$ and the norm of its derivative satisfies $\\|f'(x)\\| \\leq C$ for all $x \\in (a, b)$, then for every $x \\in [a, b]$, the inequality $\\|f(x) - f(a)\\| \\leq C(x - a)$ holds.\n[3] trapezoidal_error_le_of_c2  (theorem)\n    {f : ℝ → ℝ} {a b : ℝ} (h_f_c2 : ContDiffOn ℝ 2 f [[a, b]]) {ζ : ℝ}\n  (fpp_bound : ∀ x, |iteratedDerivWithin 2 f [[a, b]] x| ≤ ζ) {N : ℕ} (N_nonzero : 0 < N) :\n  |trapezoidal_error f N a b| ≤ |b - a| ^ 3 * ζ / (12 * N ^ 2)\n    If a function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ is twice continuously differentiable on the interval $[a, b]$ and its second derivative is bounded in absolute value by $ \\zeta $, then the trapezoidal rule error for approximating $ \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx $ with $ N $ subintervals satisfies $ |\\text{trapezoidal error}| \\leq \\frac{|b - a|^3 \\zeta}{12 N^2} $.\n[4] image_norm_le_of_liminf_right_slope_norm_lt_deriv_boundary  (theorem)\n    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] {f : ℝ → E} {f' : ℝ → ℝ}\n  (hf : ContinuousOn f (Icc a b))\n    -- `hf'` actually says `liminf (‖f z‖ - ‖f x‖) / (z - x) ≤ f' x`\n    \n  (hf' : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ∀ r, f' x < r → ∃ᶠ z in 𝓝[>] x, slope (norm ∘ f) x z < r) {B B' : ℝ → ℝ} (ha : ‖f a‖ ≤ B a)\n  (hB : ContinuousOn B (Icc a b)) (hB' : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, HasDerivWithinAt B (B' x) (Ici x) x)\n  (bound : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ‖f x‖ = B x → f' x < B' x) : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ Icc a b → ‖f x‖ ≤ B x\n    Let $ E $ be a normed additive commutative group, $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ a function continuous on $[a, b]$, and $ f' : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ a function satisfying: for every $ x \\in (a, b] $ and $ r \\in \\mathbb{R} $, if $ f'(x) < r $, then there exists a right neighborhood of $ x $ where the slope $ \\frac{\\|f(z)\\| - \\|f(x)\\|}{z - x} < r $. Given continuous functions $ B, B' : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ on $[a, b]$ such that $ B' $ is the derivative of $ B $ at each $ x \\in (a, b] $, and $ \\|f(x)\\| = B(x) $ implies $ f'(x) < B'(x) $, it follows that $ \\|f(x)\\| \\leq B(x) $ for all $ x \\...\n[5] image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_le_deriv_boundary  (theorem)\n    {f' : ℝ → E} (hf : ContinuousOn f (Icc a b)) (hf' : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, HasDerivWithinAt f (f' x) (Ici x) x) {B B' : ℝ → ℝ}\n  (ha : ‖f a‖ ≤ B a) (hB : ∀ x, HasDerivAt B (B' x) x) (bound : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ‖f' x‖ ≤ B' x) :\n  ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ Icc a b → ‖f x‖ ≤ B x\n    If $ f $ is continuous on $[a, b]$, differentiable on $[a, b)$ with derivative $ f' $, and $\\|f(a)\\| \\leq B(a)$, where $ B $ is differentiable with derivative $ B' $, and $\\|f'(x)\\| \\leq B'(x)$ for all $ x \\in [a, b) $, then $\\|f(x)\\| \\leq B(x)$ for all $ x \\in [a, b] $.\n[6] image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_lt_deriv_boundary  (theorem)\n    {f' : ℝ → E} (hf : ContinuousOn f (Icc a b)) (hf' : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, HasDerivWithinAt f (f' x) (Ici x) x) {B B' : ℝ → ℝ}\n  (ha : ‖f a‖ ≤ B a) (hB : ∀ x, HasDerivAt B (B' x) x) (bound : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ‖f x‖ = B x → ‖f' x‖ < B' x) :\n  ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ Icc a b → ‖f x‖ ≤ B x\n    Let $ f : [a, b] o E $ be a function continuous on the closed interval $[a, b]$ and differentiable on the open interval $(a, b)$, where $ E $ is a normed vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$. Suppose there exist real-valued functions $ B $ and $ B' $ such that: 1. $ \\|f(a)\\| \\leq B(a) $, 2. $ B $ is differentiable with derivative $ B' $, 3. For all $ x \\in (a, b) $, if $ \\|f(x)\\| = B(x) $, then $ \\|f'(x)\\| < B'(x) $. Then, for all $ x \\in [a, b] $, it holds that $ \\|f(x)\\| \\leq B(x) $.\n[7] image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_le_deriv_boundary'  (theorem)\n    {f' : ℝ → E} (hf : ContinuousOn f (Icc a b)) (hf' : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, HasDerivWithinAt f (f' x) (Ici x) x) {B B' : ℝ → ℝ}\n  (ha : ‖f a‖ ≤ B a) (hB : ContinuousOn B (Icc a b)) (hB' : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, HasDerivWithinAt B (B' x) (Ici x) x)\n  (bound : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ‖f' x‖ ≤ B' x) : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ Icc a b → ‖f x‖ ≤ B x\n    Let $ f : [a, b] \\to E $ be a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, and suppose that for each $ x \\in (a, b] $, $ f $ has a right derivative at $ x $ with norm $ \\|f'(x)\\| \\leq B'(x) $. Let $ B : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{R} $ be a continuous function such that $ B $ is right-differentiable on $ (a, b] $ with derivative $ B'(x) $, and assume $ \\|f(a)\\| \\leq B(a) $. Then, for all $ x \\in [a, b] $, it holds that $ \\|f(x)\\| \\leq B(x) $.\n[8] norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le_segment'  (theorem)\n    {f' : ℝ → E} {C : ℝ} (hf : ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, HasDerivWithinAt f (f' x) (Icc a b) x) (bound : ∀ x ∈ Ico a b, ‖f' x‖ ≤ C) :\n  ∀ x ∈ Icc a b, ‖f x - f a‖ ≤ C * (x - a)\n    Let $ E $ be a normed vector space over $ \\mathbb{R} $, and let $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ be a function differentiable on the closed interval $ [a, b] $ with derivative $ f'(x) $ satisfying $ \\|f'(x)\\| \\leq C $ for all $ x \\in (a, b) $. Then for every $ x \\in [a, b] $, the inequality $ \\|f(x) - f(a)\\| \\leq C \\cdot (x - a) $ holds. This is a mean value inequality bounding the difference of function values by the product of the derivative bound and the interval length.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"ContDiffOn iteratedDerivWithin second derivative\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] iteratedDerivWithin_vcomp_two  (theorem)\n    (hg : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 2 g t (f x)) (hf : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 2 f s x) (ht : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 t) (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 s)\n  (hx : x ∈ s) (hst : MapsTo f s t) :\n  iteratedDerivWithin 2 (g ∘ f) s x =\n    iteratedFDerivWithin 𝕜 2 g t (f x) (fun _ ↦ derivWithin f s x) +\n      fderivWithin 𝕜 g t (f x) (iteratedDerivWithin 2 f s x)\n    Let $ E $, $ F $, and $ G $ be normed vector spaces over a nontrivially normed field $ \\mathbb{K} $. Suppose $ f: E \\to F $ is twice continuously differentiable within a set $ s \\subseteq E $ at $ x \\in s $, $ g: F \\to G $ is twice continuously differentiable within a set $ t \\subseteq F $ at $ f(x) $, and $ f $ maps $ s $ into $ t $. If $ s $ and $ t $ have unique differentiability structures, then the second iterated Fréchet derivative of $ g \\circ f $ at $ x $ within $ s $ satisfies: $$ \\mathrm{iteratedDerivWithin}_2(g \\circ f, s)(x) = \\mathrm{iteratedFDerivWithin}_2(g, t)(f(x))\\left(\\mathr...\n[2] iteratedDerivWithin_succ'  (theorem)\n    {x : 𝕜} : iteratedDerivWithin (n + 1) f s x = (iteratedDerivWithin n (derivWithin f s) s) x\n    The $(n+1)$th derivative of $f$ within the set $s$ at $x$ is equal to the $n$th derivative of the derivative of $f$ within $s$, evaluated at $x$.\n[3] iteratedDerivWithin_scomp_two  (theorem)\n    (hg : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 2 g t (f x)) (hf : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 2 f s x) (ht : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 t) (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 s)\n  (hx : x ∈ s) (hst : MapsTo f s t) :\n  iteratedDerivWithin 2 (g ∘ f) s x =\n    derivWithin f s x ^ 2 • iteratedDerivWithin 2 g t (f x) + iteratedDerivWithin 2 f s x • derivWithin g t (f x)\n    If $ f $ is twice continuously differentiable within $ s $ at $ x $, $ g $ is twice continuously differentiable within $ t $ at $ f(x) $, and $ s $, $ t $ satisfy unique differentiability conditions, then the second iterated derivative of $ g \\circ f $ at $ x $ within $ s $ satisfies: $$ \\partial^2(g \\circ f)(x) = (\\partial f(x))^2 \\cdot \\partial^2 g(f(x)) + \\partial^2 f(x) \\cdot \\partial g(f(x)). $$\n[4] iteratedDerivWithin_comp_two  (theorem)\n    (hg : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 2 g t (f x)) (hf : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 2 f s x) (ht : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 t) (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 s)\n  (hx : x ∈ s) (hst : MapsTo f s t) :\n  iteratedDerivWithin 2 (g ∘ f) s x =\n    iteratedDerivWithin 2 g t (f x) * derivWithin f s x ^ 2 + derivWithin g t (f x) * iteratedDerivWithin 2 f s x\n    Let $\\mathbb{K}$ be a normed field and $F$ a normed vector space over $\\mathbb{K}$. Suppose $f : \\mathbb{K} \\to \\mathbb{K}$ is twice continuously differentiable within a set $s \\subseteq \\mathbb{K}$ containing $x$, and $g : \\mathbb{K} \\to F$ is twice continuously differentiable within $t = f(s)$. If $s$ and $t$ satisfy unique differentiability conditions, then the second iterated derivative of the composition $g \\circ f$ at $x$ within $s$ satisfies: $$ D^2(g \\circ f)(x) = D^2g(f(x)) \\cdot (Df(x))^2 + Dg(f(x)) \\cdot D^2f(x). $$ This generalizes the chain rule to second-order derivatives in the...\n[5] ContDiffWithinAt.differentiableWithinAt_iteratedDerivWithin  (theorem)\n    {n : WithTop ℕ∞} {m : ℕ} (h : ContDiffWithinAt 𝕜 n f s x) (hmn : m < n) (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 (insert x s)) :\n  DifferentiableWithinAt 𝕜 (iteratedDerivWithin m f s) s x\n    If a function $ f $ is continuously differentiable of order $ n $ within a set $ s $ at a point $ x $, and $ m < n $, and the set $ s $ with $ x $ satisfies the unique differentiability condition, then the $ m $-th iterated derivative of $ f $ within $ s $ is differentiable at $ x $ within $ s $.\n[6] ContDiffOn.differentiableOn_iteratedDerivWithin  (theorem)\n    {n : WithTop ℕ∞} {m : ℕ} (h : ContDiffOn 𝕜 n f s) (hmn : m < n) (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 s) :\n  DifferentiableOn 𝕜 (iteratedDerivWithin m f s) s\n    Let $ \\mathbb{K} $ be a nontrivially normed field, and let $ E $, $ F $ be normed vector spaces over $ \\mathbb{K} $. Suppose $ f : E \\to F $ is continuously differentiable of order $ n $ on a set $ s \\subseteq E $, where $ s $ satisfies the unique differentiability condition. For any natural number $ m $ with $ m < n $, the $ m $-th iterated derivative of $ f $ within $ s $ is differentiable on $ s $.\n[7] iteratedFDerivWithin_two_apply'  (theorem)\n    (f : E → F) {z : E} (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 s) (hz : z ∈ s) (v w : E) :\n  iteratedFDerivWithin 𝕜 2 f s z ![v, w] = fderivWithin 𝕜 (fderivWithin 𝕜 f s) s z v w\n    For a function $ f:E\\to F $ differentiable within a set $ s\\subseteq E $ at a point $ z\\in s $ where $ s $ has unique differentiability, the second iterated Fréchet derivative of $ f $ at $ z $ satisfies $ D^2f(z)(v,w) = D(Df(z))(v)(w) $ for all $ v,w\\in E $. This equates the second derivative to the derivative of the first derivative, both evaluated within the set $ s $.\n[8] iteratedFDerivWithin_two_apply  (theorem)\n    (f : E → F) {z : E} (hs : UniqueDiffOn 𝕜 s) (hz : z ∈ s) (m : Fin 2 → E) :\n  iteratedFDerivWithin 𝕜 2 f s z m = fderivWithin 𝕜 (fderivWithin 𝕜 f s) s z (m 0) (m 1)\n    For a function $ f: E o F $ between normed vector spaces over a nontrivially normed field $ \\mathbb{K} $, if $ s \\subseteq E $ has unique differentiability at $ z \\in s $, then the second iterated Fréchet derivative of $ f $ at $ z $ within $ s $ evaluated at vectors $ v, w \\in E $ equals the Fréchet derivative of the first derivative $ Df|_s(z) $ applied to $ v $, then evaluated at $ w $. Formally, $ D^2f|_s(z)(v,w) = D(Df|_s(z))(v)(w) $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"landau inequality derivative bound\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Complex.norm_deriv_le_of_forall_mem_sphere_norm_le  (theorem)\n    {c : ℂ} {R C : ℝ} {f : ℂ → F} (hR : 0 < R) (hd : DiffContOnCl ℂ f (ball c R)) (hC : ∀ z ∈ sphere c R, ‖f z‖ ≤ C) :\n  ‖deriv f c‖ ≤ C / R\n    If a function $ f : \\mathbb{C} \\to F $ is differentiable and continuous on the closed ball of radius $ R > 0 $ centered at $ c \\in \\mathbb{C} $, and if $ \\|f(z)\\| \\leq C $ for all $ z $ on the boundary sphere of this ball, then the norm of the derivative of $ f $ at $ c $ satisfies $ \\|f'(c)\\| \\leq \\frac{C}{R} $.\n[2] Real.norm_deriv_mulExpNegMulSq_le_one  (theorem)\n    (hε : 0 < ε) (x : ℝ) : ‖deriv (mulExpNegMulSq ε) x‖ ≤ 1\n    For any $ \\varepsilon > 0 $ and real number $ x $, the absolute value of the derivative of the function $ f(x) = x e^{-\\varepsilon x^2} $ satisfies $ |f'(x)| \\leq 1 $.\n[3] RightDerivMeasurableAux.norm_sub_le_of_mem_A  (theorem)\n    {r x : ℝ} (hr : 0 < r) (ε : ℝ) {L₁ L₂ : F} (h₁ : x ∈ A f L₁ r ε) (h₂ : x ∈ A f L₂ r ε) : ‖L₁ - L₂‖ ≤ 4 * ε\n    For a function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to F $ into a normed vector space $ F $, and given $ r, \\varepsilon \\in \\mathbb{R}_{>0} $, if $ x $ belongs to the auxiliary set $ A(f, L_1, r, \\varepsilon) $ and $ A(f, L_2, r, \\varepsilon) $, then the norm of the difference between $ L_1 $ and $ L_2 $ satisfies $ \\|L_1 - L_2\\| \\l