{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nThe diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. After four hours, about how many more miles has Alberto biked than Bjorn?\n\n$\\text{(A)}\\ 15 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 20 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 25 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 30 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 35$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"distance rate time problem difference miles bikers\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.dist_eq  (theorem)\n    (x y : ℝ) : dist x y = |x - y|\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, the distance between $x$ and $y$ is equal to the absolute value of their difference, i.e., $d(x, y) = |x - y|$.\n[2] NNReal.dist_eq  (theorem)\n    (a b : ℝ≥0) : dist a b = |(a : ℝ) - b|\n    For any two non-negative real numbers $a$ and $b$, the distance between them in the pseudo-metric space structure of $\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}$ is equal to the absolute value of their difference when considered as real numbers, i.e., $\\mathrm{dist}(a, b) = |a - b|$.\n[3] NormedAddTorsor.dist_eq_norm'  (theorem)\n    ∀ {V : outParam (Type u_1)} {P : Type u_2} {inst : SeminormedAddCommGroup V} {inst_1 : PseudoMetricSpace P}\n  [self : NormedAddTorsor V P] (x y : P), dist x y = ‖x -ᵥ y‖\n    In a normed additive torsor, for any two points $x$ and $y$, the distance between $x$ and $y$ is equal to the norm of the vector difference $x - y$.\n[4] SeminormedAddGroup.dist_eq  (theorem)\n    ∀ {E : Type u_8} [self : SeminormedAddGroup E] (x y : E), dist x y = ‖x - y‖\n    For any elements $x$ and $y$ in a seminormed additive group $E$, the distance between $x$ and $y$ is equal to the norm of $x - y$, i.e., $d(x, y) = \\|x - y\\|$.\n[5] NormedAddCommGroup.dist_eq  (theorem)\n    ∀ {E : Type u_8} [self : NormedAddCommGroup E] (x y : E), dist x y = ‖x - y‖\n    For any elements $x$ and $y$ in a normed additive commutative group $E$, the distance between $x$ and $y$ is equal to the norm of $x - y$, i.e., $d(x, y) = \\|x - y\\|$.\n[6] Int.dist_eq'  (theorem)\n    (m n : ℤ) : dist m n = |m - n|\n    For any integers $m$ and $n$, the distance between $m$ and $n$ is equal to the absolute value of their difference, i.e., $d(m, n) = |m - n|$.\n[7] NormedAddGroup.dist_eq  (theorem)\n    ∀ {E : Type u_8} [self : NormedAddGroup E] (x y : E), dist x y = ‖x - y‖\n    In a normed additive group $E$, the distance between any two elements $x$ and $y$ is equal to the norm of their difference, i.e., $d(x, y) = \\|x - y\\|$.\n[8] Int.dist_eq  (theorem)\n    (x y : ℤ) : dist x y = |(x : ℝ) - y|\n    For any integers $x$ and $y$, the distance between $x$ and $y$ is equal to the absolute value of their difference, i.e., $\\text{dist}(x, y) = |x - y|$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"AMC math competition Alberto Bjorn bikers miles graph diagram problem\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] 1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 4 - AoPS Wiki\n    The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. After four hours, about how many more miles has Alberto biked than Bjorn? d 60. Thus the ...\n    https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_4&srsltid=AfmBOorm_r9HwA3C-saV2yuM12YOE3_5zp7QDApi-y24_ydhvVsnxomN\n[2] AMC 8 Problems 2022.pdf\n    This is a 25-question multiple-choice competition. Figures are not necessarily drawn to scale. The problems and solutions for this AMC 8\n    https://981338.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hubfs/981338/Blogs/AMC%208%20Problems%20and%20Solutions/AMC%208%20Problems%202022.pdf\n[3] AMC8sample8.pdf - American Math Competition 8 Practice ...\n    3.Mark your answertoeach problem on the AMC8Answer Form with a #2pencil.Check the blackened circles for accuracy and erase errors and stray ...\n    https://www.coursehero.com/file/181237626/AMC8sample8pdf/\n[4] 2017 AMC 8 Math Contest Problems | PDF | Mathematics\n    The document contains the 2017 AMC 8 math competition problems, which include various mathematical concepts such as arithmetic, geometry, probability, and ...\n    https://www.scribd.com/document/838627862/amc8-2017-exam\n[5] Math Olympiad Problems: AMC 10 with Solutions\n    The area of the shaded region in the diagram is of the area of the unshaded region. What is the radian measure of the acute angle formed by the two lines? (Note ...\n    https://www.cliffsnotes.com/study-notes/21256867\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"1999 AMC 8 Problem 4 Alberto Bjorn bikers miles graph solution\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] 1999 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 4 - AoPS Wiki\n    The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. Bjorn biked 45 miles, and Alberto biked 60. Thus the answer is $60-45=15$\n    https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_4&srsltid=AfmBOorwtQWM7fDxg4auS0j-3dtjcIU5elM5px64GPzwGC8JD95IXR_5\n[2] Problem 4 (1999 AMC 8)\n    The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. After four hours, about how many more miles has Alberto biked than Bjorn?\n    https://www.usamoguide.com/problems/amc-8-problem-4-1999-amc-8-amc8-1999-4\n[3] 1999 AMC8 - Problems & Solutions\n    Problem 4: The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. After four hours about how many more miles has Alberto biked then Bjorn?\n    https://wiki.randommath.com/amc8/1999\n[4] 1999 AMC 8 — Problems & Online Timed Exam\n    Difficulty rating: 660. Solution: Looking at the graph, we have that Alberto has biked 60 60 60 miles and Bjorn 45 45 45 miles after 4 4 4 hours.\n    https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/1999\n[5] 1999 AMC 8 Problem Solutions | PDF\n    Solution. Problem 4. The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. After four hours, about how many more miles has Alberto biked than Bjorn?\n    https://www.scribd.com/document/703431435/Art-of-Problem-Solving\n</tool_response>\nBased on the search results, I can see this is the 1999 AMC 8 Problem 4. The problem involves a graph showing miles traveled by two bikers Alberto and Bjorn over time. After 4 hours, Alberto has biked 60 miles and Bjorn has biked 45 miles, so the difference is 15 miles (answer A).\n\nSince this problem refers to a specific diagram/graph that provides the data, I need to formalize this by encoding the given information from the graph (that after 4 hours, Alberto biked 60 miles and Bjorn biked 45 miles) and then state that the difference is 15 miles.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \n1999 AMC 8 Problem 4: The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn.\nAfter four hours, Alberto has biked 60 miles and Bjorn has biked 45 miles.\nThe problem asks how many more miles Alberto has biked than Bjorn.\nThe answer is 15 miles.\n-/\ntheorem amc8_1999_problem4 : \n  let alberto_miles_after_4_hours : ℕ := 60\n  let bjorn_miles_after_4_hours : ℕ := 45\n  alberto_miles_after_4_hours - bjorn_miles_after_4_hours = 15 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the difference in miles biked by Alberto and Bjorn after 4 hours. The answer is derived from their respective distances (60 vs 45 miles) leading to a difference of 15. The question expects an exact numerical answer from given data.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): Defines alberto_miles_after_4_hours = 60 and bjorn_miles_after_4_hours = 45 (both ℕ). The theorem asserts their difference equals 15. Uses natural numbers and direct arithmetic.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement precisely encodes the numerical values (60 and 45) and computes the difference (15). The problem's \"about\" phrasing is compatible with the exact answer since AMC problems expect precise answers from given data. No missing constraints or variables.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean theorem exactly formalizes the problem's numerical setup and required computation.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match - variables correspond to bikers' distances, arithmetic operation matches the question's requirement, and the answer value (15) aligns with the problem's correct option.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \n1999 AMC 8 Problem 4: The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn.\nAfter four hours, Alberto has biked 60 miles and Bjorn has biked 45 miles.\nThe problem asks how many more miles Alberto has biked than Bjorn.\nThe answer is 15 miles.\n-/\ntheorem amc8_1999_problem4 : \n  let alberto_miles_after_4_hours : ℕ := 60\n  let bjorn_miles_after_4_hours : ℕ := 45\n  alberto_miles_after_4_hours - bjorn_miles_after_4_hours = 15 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "The diagram shows the miles traveled by bikers Alberto and Bjorn. After four hours, about how many more miles has Alberto biked than Bjorn?\n\n$\\text{(A)}\\ 15 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 20 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 25 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 30 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 35$", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 3, "tools_used": ["leansearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_amc_aime", "question_id": "amc_aime_202862"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nA real-valued function $f$ has the property that for all real numbers $a$ and $b,$ \\[f(a + b)  + f(a - b) = 2f(a) f(b).\\] Which one of the following cannot be the value of $f(1)?$\n$\\textbf{(A) } 0 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 1 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } -1 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 2 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } -2$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"functional equation f(a+b) + f(a-b) = 2f(a)f(b) d'Alembert cosine\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.Angle.cos  (definition)\n    (θ : Angle) : ℝ\n    The function maps a real angle $\\theta$ (modulo $2\\pi$) to the cosine of $\\theta$, which is a real number. This is well-defined because the cosine function is periodic with period $2\\pi$.\n[2] Real.cos_add_cos  (theorem)\n    : cos x + cos y = 2 * cos ((x + y) / 2) * cos ((x - y) / 2)\n    For all real numbers $x$ and $y$, the sum of their cosines is equal to twice the product of the cosine of their average and the cosine of half their difference. That is, $\\cos x + \\cos y = 2 \\cos\\left( \\frac{x + y}{2} \\right) \\cos\\left( \\frac{x - y}{2} \\right)$.\n[3] Real.cos_add  (theorem)\n    : cos (x + y) = cos x * cos y - sin x * sin y\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, the cosine of their sum $x + y$ satisfies the identity $\\cos(x + y) = \\cos x \\cos y - \\sin x \\sin y$.\n[4] Real.two_mul_cos_mul_cos  (theorem)\n    (x y : ℝ) : 2 * cos x * cos y = cos (x - y) + cos (x + y)\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, $2 \\cos x \\cos y = \\cos(x - y) + \\cos(x + y)$.\n[5] Real.cos  (definition)\n    (x : ℝ) : ℝ\n    The function maps a real number $ x $ to the real part of the complex cosine of $ x $.\n[6] Complex.cos_add_cos  (theorem)\n    : cos x + cos y = 2 * cos ((x + y) / 2) * cos ((x - y) / 2)\n    For any complex numbers $x$ and $y$, the sum of their cosines satisfies $\\cos x + \\cos y = 2\\cos\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{x-y}{2}\\right)$.\n[7] Real.cos_two_mul  (theorem)\n    : cos (2 * x) = 2 * cos x ^ 2 - 1\n    For any real number $x$, $\\cos(2x) = 2\\cos^2 x - 1$.\n[8] Real.cos_two_mul'  (theorem)\n    : cos (2 * x) = cos x ^ 2 - sin x ^ 2\n    For any real number $x$, the cosine of twice $x$ is equal to the square of the cosine of $x$ minus the square of the sine of $x$, i.e., $\\cos(2x) = \\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"d'Alembert functional equation f(x+y) + f(x-y) = 2f(x)f(y) solutions cosine hyperbolic\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.cosh  (definition)\n    (x : ℝ) : ℝ\n    The function maps a real number $ x $ to $ \\cosh(x) $, defined as the real part of the complex hyperbolic cosine $ \\cosh(x + 0i) = \\frac{e^{x} + e^{-x}}{2} $.\n[2] Complex.cosh  (definition)\n    (z : ℂ) : ℂ\n    The hyperbolic cosine function for complex numbers, defined by $ \\cosh(z) = \\frac{e^z + e^{-z}}{2} $.\n[3] Real.cosh_add  (theorem)\n    : cosh (x + y) = cosh x * cosh y + sinh x * sinh y\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, the hyperbolic cosine of the sum $x + y$ satisfies the identity $\\cosh(x + y) = \\cosh x \\cosh y + \\sinh x \\sinh y$.\n[4] Real.cosh_eq  (theorem)\n    (x : ℝ) : cosh x = (exp x + exp (-x)) / 2\n    For any real number $x$, the hyperbolic cosine function satisfies $\\cosh(x) = \\frac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}$.\n[5] Complex.cosh_add  (theorem)\n    : cosh (x + y) = cosh x * cosh y + sinh x * sinh y\n    For all complex numbers $x$ and $y$, the hyperbolic cosine of $x + y$ satisfies $\\cosh(x + y) = \\cosh x \\cosh y + \\sinh x \\sinh y$.\n[6] Real.cosh_sub  (theorem)\n    : cosh (x - y) = cosh x * cosh y - sinh x * sinh y\n    For all real numbers $x$ and $y$, the hyperbolic cosine of the difference $x - y$ satisfies $\\cosh(x - y) = \\cosh x \\cdot \\cosh y - \\sinh x \\cdot \\sinh y$.\n[7] Real.cosh_two_mul  (theorem)\n    : cosh (2 * x) = cosh x ^ 2 + sinh x ^ 2\n    For any real number $ x $, the hyperbolic cosine of $ 2x $ satisfies $ \\cosh(2x) = \\cosh^2 x + \\sinh^2 x $.\n[8] Complex.cosh_sub  (theorem)\n    : cosh (x - y) = cosh x * cosh y - sinh x * sinh y\n    For all complex numbers $x$ and $y$, the hyperbolic cosine of $x - y$ satisfies $\\cosh(x - y) = \\cosh x \\cdot \\cosh y - \\sinh x \\cdot \\sinh y$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"exists function satisfying f(a+b) + f(a-b) = 2*f(a)*f(b)\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] AddChar.mk.noConfusion  (definition)\n    {A : Type u_1} →\n  {inst : AddMonoid A} →\n    {M : Type u_2} →\n      {inst_1 : Monoid M} →\n        {P : Sort u} →\n          {toFun : A → M} →\n            {map_zero_eq_one' : toFun 0 = 1} →\n              {map_add_eq_mul' : ∀ (a b : A), toFun (a + b) = toFun a * toFun b} →\n                {toFun' : A → M} →\n                  {map_zero_eq_one'' : toFun' 0 = 1} →\n                    {map_add_eq_mul'' : ∀ (a b : A), toFun' (a + b) = toFun' a * toFun' b} →\n                      { toFun := toFun, ma...\n    The type of homomorphisms from the additive monoid $ A $ to the multiplicative monoid $ M $, preserving the monoid structure: for all $ a, b \\in A $, $ f(0) = 1 $ and $ f(a + b) = f(a) \\cdot f(b) $.\n[2] WeakFEPair.f  (definition)\n    {E : Type u_1} → [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] → [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℂ E] → WeakFEPair E → ℝ → E\n    Given a weak functional equation pair consisting of a normed additive commutative group $ E $ over $ \\mathbb{C} $, this function maps a real number $ t $ to an element of $ E $. It is a component of the structure encoding data for abstract L-series functional equations.\n[3] Function.Even  (definition)\n    (f : α → β) : Prop\n    A function $ f: \\alpha \\to \\beta $ is even if $ f(-a) = f(a) $ for all $ a \\in \\alpha $.\n[4] exists_apply_eq  (theorem)\n    (a : α) (b : β) : ∃ f : α → β, f a = b\n    For any elements $a \\in A$ and $b \\in B$, there exists a function $f : A \\to B$ such that $f(a) = b$.\n[5] exists_apply_eq_apply2'  (theorem)\n    {α β γ} {f : α → β → γ} {a : α} {b : β} : ∃ x y, f a b = f x y\n    For any function $f : \\alpha \\times \\beta \\to \\gamma$ and elements $a \\in \\alpha$, $b \\in \\beta$, there exist elements $x \\in \\alpha$ and $y \\in \\beta$ such that $f(a, b) = f(x, y)$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"cosh range values cosh x >= 1\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"functional equation f(a+b) + f(a-b) = 2f(a)f(b)\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] WeakFEPair.g  (definition)\n    {E : Type u_1} → [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] → [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℂ E] → WeakFEPair E → ℝ → E\n    For a weak functional equation pair over the complex numbers, this function maps real numbers to elements in the associated normed additive commutative group $ E $, which is equipped with a normed vector space structure over $ \\mathbb{C} $.\n[2] WeakFEPair.f  (definition)\n    {E : Type u_1} → [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] → [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℂ E] → WeakFEPair E → ℝ → E\n    Given a weak functional equation pair consisting of a normed additive commutative group $ E $ over $ \\mathbb{C} $, this function maps a real number $ t $ to an element of $ E $. It is a component of the structure encoding data for abstract L-series functional equations.\n[3] QuadraticMap.polar_add_left_iff  (theorem)\n    {f : M → N} {x x' y : M} :\n  polar f (x + x') y = polar f x y + polar f x' y ↔\n    f (x + x' + y) + (f x + f x' + f y) = f (x + x') + f (x' + y) + f (y + x)\n    For a quadratic map $ f : M \\to N $ and elements $ x, x', y \\in M $, the polar form $ B(x, y) = f(x + y) - f(x) - f(y) $ satisfies $ B(x + x', y) = B(x, y) + B(x', y) $ if and only if $ f(x + x' + y) + f(x) + f(x') + f(y) = f(x + x') + f(x' + y) + f(y + x) $.\n[4] StrongFEPair.functional_equation  (theorem)\n    (s : ℂ) : P.Λ (P.k - s) = P.ε • P.symm.Λ s\n    For any complex number $s$, the Lambda function $\\Lambda$ associated with a strong functional equation pair satisfies $\\Lambda(k - s) = \\varepsilon \\cdot \\Lambda_{\\text{symm}}(s)$, where $k$ is the real parameter of the pair, $\\varepsilon$ is its epsilon factor, and $\\Lambda_{\\text{symm}}$ denotes the Lambda function of the symmetric pair obtained by swapping the functions and inverting the epsilon factor.\n[5] QuadraticMap.polar  (definition)\n    (f : M → N) (x y : M)\n    Given a quadratic map $ f : M → N $, the polar form is the bilinear map $ B(x, y) = f(x + y) - f(x) - f(y) $, which captures the symmetric interaction between elements $ x, y ∈ M $.\n[6] WeakFEPair.h_feq'  (theorem)\n    (P : WeakFEPair E) (x : ℝ) (hx : 0 < x) : P.g (1 / x) = (P.ε⁻¹ * ↑(x ^ P.k)) • P.f x\n    For any positive real number $x$, the function $g$ evaluated at $1/x$ equals the product of $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, $x^k$, and the function $f$ evaluated at $x$, i.e., $g\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\varepsilon^{-1} \\cdot x^k \\cdot f(x)$.\n[7] WeakFEPair.h_feq  (theorem)\n    ∀ {E : Type u_1} [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℂ E] (self : WeakFEPair E),\n  ∀ x ∈ Set.Ioi 0, self.f (1 / x) = (self.ε * ↑(x ^ self.k)) • self.g x\n    For any weak functional equation pair $(E, f, g, k, \\varepsilon)$, where $E$ is a normed additive commutative group over $\\mathbb{C}$, and for every positive real number $x$, the following functional equation holds: $$ f\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\varepsilon \\cdot x^k \\cdot g(x), $$ where the multiplication is the scalar multiplication in the normed space $E$.\n[8] Function.Even  (definition)\n    (f : α → β) : Prop\n    A function $ f: \\alpha \\to \\beta $ is even if $ f(-a) = f(a) $ for all $ a \\in \\alpha $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"d'Alembert functional equation cosine f(a+b)+f(a-b)=2f(a)f(b)\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.cos_add  (theorem)\n    : cos (x + y) = cos x * cos y - sin x * sin y\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, the cosine of their sum $x + y$ satisfies the identity $\\cos(x + y) = \\cos x \\cos y - \\sin x \\sin y$.\n[2] Real.cos_add_cos  (theorem)\n    : cos x + cos y = 2 * cos ((x + y) / 2) * cos ((x - y) / 2)\n    For all real numbers $x$ and $y$, the sum of their cosines is equal to twice the product of the cosine of their average and the cosine of half their difference. That is, $\\cos x + \\cos y = 2 \\cos\\left( \\frac{x + y}{2} \\right) \\cos\\left( \\frac{x - y}{2} \\right)$.\n[3] Real.two_mul_cos_mul_cos  (theorem)\n    (x y : ℝ) : 2 * cos x * cos y = cos (x - y) + cos (x + y)\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, $2 \\cos x \\cos y = \\cos(x - y) + \\cos(x + y)$.\n[4] Real.cos_sq  (theorem)\n    : cos x ^ 2 = 1 / 2 + cos (2 * x) / 2\n    For any real number $x$, the square of the cosine function satisfies $\\cos^2 x = \\frac{1 + \\cos(2x)}{2}$.\n[5] Complex.cos_two_mul  (theorem)\n    : cos (2 * x) = 2 * cos x ^ 2 - 1\n    For any complex number $x$, the cosine of $2x$ is equal to $2\\cos^2 x - 1$, i.e., $\\cos(2x) = 2\\cos^2 x - 1$.\n[6] Real.Angle.cos  (definition)\n    (θ : Angle) : ℝ\n    The function maps a real angle $\\theta$ (modulo $2\\pi$) to the cosine of $\\theta$, which is a real number. This is well-defined because the cosine function is periodic with period $2\\pi$.\n[7] Real.Angle.cos_add  (theorem)\n    (θ₁ θ₂ : Real.Angle) : cos (θ₁ + θ₂) = cos θ₁ * cos θ₂ - sin θ₁ * sin θ₂\n    For any real angles $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$ modulo $2\\pi$, the cosine of their sum $\\theta_1 + \\theta_2$ satisfies the identity $\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2) = \\cos \\theta_1 \\cos \\theta_2 - \\sin \\theta_1 \\sin \\theta_2$. This formula leverages the periodicity of the cosine function with period $2\\pi$ to ensure well-definedness on the additive circle $\\mathbb{R}/\\langle 2\\pi \\rangle$.\n[8] Real.cos_two_mul'  (theorem)\n    : cos (2 * x) = cos x ^ 2 - sin x ^ 2\n    For any real number $x$, the cosine of twice $x$ is equal to the square of the cosine of $x$ minus the square of the sine of $x$, i.e., $\\cos(2x) = \\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"exists function f : ℝ → ℝ such that f(a+b) + f(a-b) = 2*f(a)*f(b)\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] exists_apply_eq  (theorem)\n    (a : α) (b : β) : ∃ f : α → β, f a = b\n    For any elements $a \\in A$ and $b \\in B$, there exists a function $f : A \\to B$ such that $f(a) = b$.\n[2] WeakFEPair.g  (definition)\n    {E : Type u_1} → [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] → [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℂ E] → WeakFEPair E → ℝ → E\n    For a weak functional equation pair over the complex numbers, this function maps real numbers to elements in the associated normed additive commutative group $ E $, which is equipped with a normed vector space structure over $ \\mathbb{C} $.\n[3] WeakFEPair.f  (definition)\n    {E : Type u_1} → [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] → [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℂ E] → WeakFEPair E → ℝ → E\n    Given a weak functional equation pair consisting of a normed additive commutative group $ E $ over $ \\mathbb{C} $, this function maps a real number $ t $ to an element of $ E $. It is a component of the structure encoding data for abstract L-series functional equations.\n[4] exists_apply_eq_apply2'  (theorem)\n    {α β γ} {f : α → β → γ} {a : α} {b : β} : ∃ x y, f a b = f x y\n    For any function $f : \\alpha \\times \\beta \\to \\gamma$ and elements $a \\in \\alpha$, $b \\in \\beta$, there exist elements $x \\in \\alpha$ and $y \\in \\beta$ such that $f(a, b) = f(x, y)$.\n[5] Function.Even  (definition)\n    (f : α → β) : Prop\n    A function $ f: \\alpha \\to \\beta $ is even if $ f(-a) = f(a) $ for all $ a \\in \\alpha $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I understand the problem. The functional equation $f(a+b) + f(a-b) = 2f(a)f(b)$ is known as d'Alembert's functional equation (or the cosine functional equation). The solutions include:\n- $f(x) = 0$ (gives $f(1) = 0$)\n- $f(x) = \\cos(kx)$ for some $k$ (gives $f(1) = \\cos(k)$ which can be any value in $[-1, 1]$)\n- $f(x) = \\cosh(kx)$ for some $k$ (gives $f(1) = \\cosh(k) \\geq 1$)\n\nSo $f(1)$ can be $0$, $1$, $-1$, or any value $\\geq 1$ (from cosh), but cannot be strictly less than $-1$ except $-1$ itself. So $f(1) = -2$ is impossible.\n\nLet me formalize this as a theorem stating that if $f$ satisfies the functional equation, then $f(1) \\neq -2$.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem functional_equation_f1_not_neg_two :\n  ∀ f : ℝ → ℝ, (∀ a b : ℝ, f (a + b) + f (a - b) = 2 * f a * f b) → f 1 ≠ -2 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem defines a real-valued function $f$ satisfying the functional equation $f(a+b) + f(a-b) = 2f(a)f(b)$ for all real $a, b$. It asks to identify which value among {0, 1, -1, 2, -2} cannot be $f(1)$. Mathematical analysis shows solutions are of the form $f(x) = \\cos(kx)$ or $f(x) = \\cosh(kx)$ (or $f(x)=0$), implying $f(1) \\in [-1, 1] \\cup [1, \\infty) = [-1, \\infty)$. Thus, $f(1) = -2$ is impossible.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem states `∀ f : ℝ → ℝ, (∀ a b : ℝ, f (a + b) + f (a - b) = 2 * f a * f b) → f 1 ≠ -2`. It correctly quantifies over real-valued functions, states the functional equation hypothesis, and concludes that $f(1)$ is not equal to -2. The syntax uses standard Lean 4 notation (`∀`, `→`, `ℝ`, `≠`, `*`, `+`).\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement exactly formalizes the mathematical deduction that -2 is the impossible value. The variable domains ($\\mathbb{R}$), the functional equation, and the conclusion ($f(1) \\neq -2$) align perfectly with the natural language problem and its solution. The syntax is valid Lean 4.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct and exact formalization of the mathematical problem's solution.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The function type, quantifiers, equation, and conclusion correspond precisely. The choice of proving $f(1) \\neq -2$ correctly addresses the multiple-choice question by formalizing the unique impossible option.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem functional_equation_f1_not_neg_two :\n  ∀ f : ℝ → ℝ, (∀ a b : ℝ, f (a + b) + f (a - b) = 2 * f a * f b) → f 1 ≠ -2 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "A real-valued function $f$ has the property that for all real numbers $a$ and $b,$ \\[f(a + b)  + f(a - b) = 2f(a) f(b).\\] Which one of the following cannot be the value of $f(1)?$\n$\\textbf{(A) } 0 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 1 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } -1 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 2 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } -2$", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 6, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_amc_aime", "question_id": "amc_aime_202497"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nLet  $n$  be a positive integer not divisible by  $2$  or  $3$ . Prove that for all integers  $k$ , the number  $(k+1)^n-k^n-1$  is divisible by  $k^2+k+1$ ."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"divisibility integer divides notation\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Mathlib.Tactic.Zify.natCast_dvd  (theorem)\n    (a b : Nat) : a ∣ b ↔ (a : Int) ∣ (b : Int)\n    For any natural numbers $a$ and $b$, $a$ divides $b$ if and only if the integer $a$ divides the integer $b$, i.e., $a \\mid b \\leftrightarrow (a : \\mathbb{Z}) \\mid (b : \\mathbb{Z})$.\n[2] Mathlib.Meta.NormNum.isInt_dvd_true  (theorem)\n    : {a b : ℤ} → {a' b' c : ℤ} → IsInt a a' → IsInt b b' → Int.mul a' c = b' → a ∣ b\n    If elements $a$ and $b$ in a ring correspond to integers $a'$ and $b'$ via canonical embedding, and there exists an integer $c$ such that $a' \\cdot c = b'$, then $a$ divides $b$ in the ring.\n[3] ZNum.dvd_to_int  (theorem)\n    (m n : ZNum) : (m : ℤ) ∣ n ↔ m ∣ n\n    For any ZNum elements $ m $ and $ n $, $ m $ divides $ n $ in ZNum if and only if the integer conversion of $ m $ divides the integer conversion of $ n $.\n[4] Int.dvd_div_iff_mul_dvd  (theorem)\n    (hbc : c ∣ b) : a ∣ b / c ↔ c * a ∣ b\n    Let $c$ divide $b$. Then, $a$ divides $b/c$ if and only if $ca$ divides $b$.\n[5] Num.dvd_iff_mod_eq_zero  (theorem)\n    {m n : Num} : m ∣ n ↔ n % m = 0\n    For any binary natural numbers $m$ and $n$, $m$ divides $n$ if and only if the remainder when $n$ is divided by $m$ is zero, i.e., $n \\bmod m = 0$.\n[6] Int.dvd_natCast  (theorem)\n    {n : ℕ} : m ∣ (n : ℤ) ↔ m.natAbs ∣ n\n    For any integer $ m $ and natural number $ n $, $ m $ divides the integer cast of $ n $ if and only if the absolute value of $ m $ divides $ n $. Symbolically, $ m \\mid (n : \\mathbb{Z}) \\leftrightarrow |m| \\mid n $.\n[7] ZNum.dvd_iff_mod_eq_zero  (theorem)\n    {m n : ZNum} : m ∣ n ↔ n % m = 0\n    For any signed binary integers $ m $ and $ n $, $ m $ divides $ n $ if and only if $ n \\mod m = 0 $.\n[8] Int.natCast_dvd  (theorem)\n    {m : ℕ} : (m : ℤ) ∣ n ↔ m ∣ n.natAbs\n    For any natural number $ m $ and integer $ n $, the integer $ m $ divides $ n $ if and only if $ m $ divides the absolute value of $ n $, i.e., $ m \\mid n \\iff m \\mid |n| $.\n</tool_response>\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- Let n be a positive integer not divisible by 2 or 3. \n    Then for all integers k, (k+1)^n - k^n - 1 is divisible by k^2 + k + 1. -/\ntheorem divisibility_by_quadratic (n : ℕ) (hn_pos : 0 < n) (hn_not_div_2 : ¬(2 ∣ n)) (hn_not_div_3 : ¬(3 ∣ n)) :\n  ∀ k : ℤ, (k^2 + k + 1) ∣ ((k + 1)^n - k^n - 1) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: \n   - n is a positive integer (n ∈ ℕ, n ≥ 1).\n   - n is not divisible by 2 or 3 (¬(2 ∣ n) ∧ ¬(3 ∣ n)).\n   - For all integers k, (k+1)^n - k^n - 1 is divisible by k² + k + 1 (∀k ∈ ℤ, (k² + k + 1) ∣ ((k+1)^n - k^n - 1)).\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored):\n   - n : ℕ with 0 < n (ensures n is positive).\n   - ¬(2 ∣ n) and ¬(3 ∣ n) (matches \"not divisible by 2 or 3\").\n   - ∀ k : ℤ, (k^2 + k + 1) ∣ ((k + 1)^n - k^n - 1) (matches the divisibility claim for all integers k).\n\n3. Comparative Verification:\n   - n's type and positivity: ℕ with 0 < n correctly captures \"positive integer\".\n   - Divisibility constraints on n: ¬(2 ∣ n) and ¬(3 ∣ n) match the problem's requirements.\n   - Quantifier ∀ k : ℤ matches \"for all integers k\".\n   - Divisibility expression (k^2 + k + 1) ∣ ((k + 1)^n - k^n - 1) matches the problem's conclusion.\n   - No missing elements or unjustified additions.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement aligns perfectly with the mathematical problem's structure, constraints, and conclusion.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The Lean statement correctly formalizes the problem's requirements.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- Let n be a positive integer not divisible by 2 or 3. \n    Then for all integers k, (k+1)^n - k^n - 1 is divisible by k^2 + k + 1. -/\ntheorem divisibility_by_quadratic (n : ℕ) (hn_pos : 0 < n) (hn_not_div_2 : ¬(2 ∣ n)) (hn_not_div_3 : ¬(3 ∣ n)) :\n  ∀ k : ℤ, (k^2 + k + 1) ∣ ((k + 1)^n - k^n - 1) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Let  $n$  be a positive integer not divisible by  $2$  or  $3$ . Prove that for all integers  $k$ , the number  $(k+1)^n-k^n-1$  is divisible by  $k^2+k+1$ .", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 1, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "aops", "question_id": "21582"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nIf $A$, $B$, and $C$ represent three distinct digits from 1 to 9 and they satisfy the following equations, what is the value of the sum $A+B+C$? (In the equation below, $AA$ represents a two-digit number both of whose digits are $A$.) $$A+B=C$$$$AA-B=2\\times C$$$$C\\times B=AA+A$$ Show that it is 8."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"distinct digits natural numbers 1 to 9\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.injOn_ofDigits  (theorem)\n    {b : ℕ} (hb : 1 < b) (l : ℕ) : Set.InjOn (ofDigits b) {L : List ℕ | L.length = l ∧ ∀ x ∈ L, x < b}\n    For any integer $ b > 1 $ and natural number $ l $, the function that maps a list of digits (each less than $ b $) of length $ l $ to the natural number obtained by interpreting the list as digits in base $ b $ with the first element as the least significant digit is injective. That is, distinct such lists yield distinct numbers.\n[2] Nat.digits_lt_base  (theorem)\n    {b m d : ℕ} (hb : 1 < b) (hd : d ∈ digits b m) : d < b\n    For any base $ b > 1 $ and natural number $ m $, every digit $ d $ in the base-$ b $ representation of $ m $ satisfies $ d < b $.\n[3] Nat.digits.injective  (theorem)\n    (b : ℕ) : Function.Injective b.digits\n    For any natural number base $ b $, the function mapping a natural number $ n $ to its list of digits in base $ b $ is injective. That is, if two natural numbers have the same digit representation in base $ b $, then they must be equal.\n[4] Nat.digits_one  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : digits 1 n = List.replicate n 1\n    For any natural number $ n $, the digit representation of $ n $ in base 1 is a list containing $ n $ copies of the digit 1, i.e., $ \\text{Nat.digits}\\, 1\\, n = [\\underbrace{1, 1, \\ldots, 1}_{n \\text{ times}}] $.\n[5] Nat.modEq_nine_digits_sum  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : n ≡ (digits 10 n).sum [MOD 9]\n    For any natural number $ n $, $ n $ is congruent modulo 9 to the sum of its decimal digits, i.e., $ n \\equiv \\sum (\\text{digits}_{10}(n)) \\pmod{9} $.\n[6] Nat.bijOn_ofDigits  (theorem)\n    {b : ℕ} (hb : 1 < b) (l : ℕ) : Set.BijOn (ofDigits b) {L : List ℕ | L.length = l ∧ ∀ x ∈ L, x < b} {n | n < b ^ l}\n    For any base $ b > 1 $ and natural number $ l $, the function `ofDigits b` is a bijection between the set of lists of length $ l $ with digits less than $ b $ and the set of natural numbers less than $ b^l $.\n[7] Nat.digit_sum_le  (theorem)\n    (p n : ℕ) : List.sum (digits p n) ≤ n\n    For any natural numbers $ p $ and $ n $, the sum of the digits of $ n $ in base $ p $ is less than or equal to $ n $.\n[8] Nat.digits_of_lt  (theorem)\n    (b x : ℕ) (hx : x ≠ 0) (hxb : x < b) : digits b x = [x]\n    For any natural numbers $ b $ and $ x $, if $ x \\neq 0 $ and $ x < b $, then the digit representation of $ x $ in base $ b $ is the singleton list $[x]$.\n</tool_response>\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nGiven three distinct digits A, B, C from 1 to 9 satisfying:\n- A + B = C\n- AA - B = 2 × C (where AA is the two-digit number 11×A)\n- C × B = AA + A (where AA is the two-digit number 11×A)\nProve that A + B + C = 8.\n-/\ntheorem digit_sum_problem : \n  ∀ (A B C : ℕ), \n    (1 ≤ A ∧ A ≤ 9) →\n    (1 ≤ B ∧ B ≤ 9) →\n    (1 ≤ C ∧ C ≤ 9) →\n    (A ≠ B ∧ B ≠ C ∧ A ≠ C) →\n    (A + B = C) →\n    (11 * A - B = 2 * C) →\n    (C * B = 11 * A + A) →\n    (A + B + C = 8) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem involves three distinct digits A, B, C (1-9) satisfying three equations: A+B=C, AA-B=2C (AA=11A), and CB=AA+A (AA=11A). The goal is to prove A+B+C=8.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem uses ∀ (A B C : ℕ) with constraints 1 ≤ A,B,C ≤ 9, distinctness (A≠B, B≠C, A≠C), and the three equations (A+B=C, 11*A - B = 2*C, C*B = 11*A + A). The conclusion is A+B+C=8.\n3. Comparative Verification: All components match. The equations are correctly translated (AA=11*A), constraints on digits (1-9) and distinctness are preserved. The logical structure (if conditions hold, then conclusion) aligns with the problem's requirement to show the sum is 8 under the given equations.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement accurately formalizes the problem's constraints, equations, and conclusion.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The Lean code correctly captures the problem's variables, constraints, equations, and target conclusion.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nGiven three distinct digits A, B, C from 1 to 9 satisfying:\n- A + B = C\n- AA - B = 2 × C (where AA is the two-digit number 11×A)\n- C × B = AA + A (where AA is the two-digit number 11×A)\nProve that A + B + C = 8.\n-/\ntheorem digit_sum_problem : \n  ∀ (A B C : ℕ), \n    (1 ≤ A ∧ A ≤ 9) →\n    (1 ≤ B ∧ B ≤ 9) →\n    (1 ≤ C ∧ C ≤ 9) →\n    (A ≠ B ∧ B ≠ C ∧ A ≠ C) →\n    (A + B = C) →\n    (11 * A - B = 2 * C) →\n    (C * B = 11 * A + A) →\n    (A + B + C = 8) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "If $A$, $B$, and $C$ represent three distinct digits from 1 to 9 and they satisfy the following equations, what is the value of the sum $A+B+C$? (In the equation below, $AA$ represents a two-digit number both of whose digits are $A$.) $$A+B=C$$$$AA-B=2\\times C$$$$C\\times B=AA+A$$ Show that it is 8.", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 1, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "minif2f_test", "question_id": "mathd_numbertheory_430"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nTwo medians of a triangle with unequal sides are $3$ inches and $6$ inches. Its area is $3 \\sqrt{15}$ square inches. The length of the third median in inches, is:\n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 4\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 3\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 3\\sqrt{6}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 6\\sqrt{6}$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"triangle median length area formula\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.dist_sq_add_dist_sq_eq_two_mul_dist_midpoint_sq_add_half_dist_sq  (theorem)\n    (a b c : P) : dist a b ^ 2 + dist a c ^ 2 = 2 * (dist a (midpoint ℝ b c) ^ 2 + (dist b c / 2) ^ 2)\n    For any points $a$, $b$, and $c$ in an affine space $P$ over the real numbers, the sum of the squares of the distances from $a$ to $b$ and from $a$ to $c$ equals twice the sum of the square of the distance from $a$ to the midpoint of $b$ and $c$ and the square of half the distance between $b$ and $c$. That is, $\\|a - b\\|^2 + \\|a - c\\|^2 = 2\\left(\\|a - m\\|^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\|b - c\\|}{2}\\right)^2\\right)$, where $m$ is the midpoint of $b$ and $c$.\n[2] Affine.Simplex.median  (definition)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : AffineSubspace k P\n    The median of an affine simplex corresponding to the vertex at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through this vertex and the centroid of the face opposite to it. Formally, for a simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $, the median at index $ i $ is the affine subspace defined by the line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n[3] Affine.Simplex.median_eq_line_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    [CharZero k] (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.median i = line[k, s.points i, s.centroid]\n    For an affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over an affine space $ P $ with a ring $ k $ of characteristic zero, the median at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $. Formally, $ s.median(i) $ equals the affine line defined by the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the opposite face.\n[4] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) : dist (t.points i) t.centroid = 2 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    In an affine triangle over the real numbers, the distance from each vertex to the centroid is twice the distance from the centroid to the midpoint of the opposite side, i.e., $\\text{dist}(v_i, c) = 2 \\cdot \\text{dist}(c, m_i)$ where $c$ is the centroid and $m_i$ is the midpoint of the side opposite vertex $v_i$.\n[5] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_faceOppositeCentroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) :\n  dist (t.points i) (t.faceOppositeCentroid i) = 3 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    For any triangle $ t $ in a real affine space and any vertex index $ i $, the distance between the $ i $-th vertex $ t_i $ and the centroid of the opposite face is three times the distance between the centroid of the triangle $ t $ and the centroid of the opposite face. Formally, $ d(t_i, c_{\\text{face}}) = 3 \\cdot d(c_{\\text{triangle}}, c_{\\text{face}}) $, where $ c_{\\text{triangle}} $ denotes the centroid of $ t $ and $ c_{\\text{face}} $ denotes the centroid of the face opposite vertex $ i $.\n[6] Affine.Simplex.point_mem_median  (theorem)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.points i ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ and any index $ i $, the vertex $ s_i $ is contained in the median of $ s $ at index $ i $, which is the affine line connecting $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $.\n[7] Affine.Simplex.median.congr_simp  (theorem)\n    ∀ {k : Type u_1} {V : Type u_2} {P : Type u_3} [inst : DivisionRing k] [inst_1 : AddCommGroup V] [inst_2 : Module k V]\n  [inst_3 : AddTorsor V P] {n : ℕ} [inst_4 : NeZero n] (s s_1 : Affine.Simplex k P n),\n  s = s_1 → ∀ (i i_1 : Fin (n + 1)), i = i_1 → s.median i = s_1.median i_1\n    If two affine simplices $ s $ and $ s_1 $ are equal and their indices $ i $ and $ i_1 $ are equal, then the median of $ s $ at index $ i $ is equal to the median of $ s_1 $ at index $ i_1 $. This holds in the context of a division ring $ k $, an additive commutative group $ V $, a $ k $-module $ V $, and an affine torsor $ P $ over $ V $, for a natural number $ n $ (nonzero).\n[8] Affine.Simplex.centroid_mem_median  (theorem)\n    [CharZero k] (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.centroid ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over a ring $ k $ with characteristic zero, the centroid of $ s $ lies in the median corresponding to the $ i $-th vertex. Specifically, given a simplex $ s $ with vertices $ s_0, s_1, \\ldots, s_n $, the centroid $ \\frac{1}{n+1}\\sum_{i=0}^n s_i $ is contained in the affine line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"triangle area from medians formula\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Affine.Simplex.median  (definition)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : AffineSubspace k P\n    The median of an affine simplex corresponding to the vertex at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through this vertex and the centroid of the face opposite to it. Formally, for a simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $, the median at index $ i $ is the affine subspace defined by the line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n[2] EuclideanGeometry.dist_sq_add_dist_sq_eq_two_mul_dist_midpoint_sq_add_half_dist_sq  (theorem)\n    (a b c : P) : dist a b ^ 2 + dist a c ^ 2 = 2 * (dist a (midpoint ℝ b c) ^ 2 + (dist b c / 2) ^ 2)\n    For any points $a$, $b$, and $c$ in an affine space $P$ over the real numbers, the sum of the squares of the distances from $a$ to $b$ and from $a$ to $c$ equals twice the sum of the square of the distance from $a$ to the midpoint of $b$ and $c$ and the square of half the distance between $b$ and $c$. That is, $\\|a - b\\|^2 + \\|a - c\\|^2 = 2\\left(\\|a - m\\|^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\|b - c\\|}{2}\\right)^2\\right)$, where $m$ is the midpoint of $b$ and $c$.\n[3] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) : dist (t.points i) t.centroid = 2 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    In an affine triangle over the real numbers, the distance from each vertex to the centroid is twice the distance from the centroid to the midpoint of the opposite side, i.e., $\\text{dist}(v_i, c) = 2 \\cdot \\text{dist}(c, m_i)$ where $c$ is the centroid and $m_i$ is the midpoint of the side opposite vertex $v_i$.\n[4] Affine.Simplex.centroid_mem_median  (theorem)\n    [CharZero k] (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.centroid ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over a ring $ k $ with characteristic zero, the centroid of $ s $ lies in the median corresponding to the $ i $-th vertex. Specifically, given a simplex $ s $ with vertices $ s_0, s_1, \\ldots, s_n $, the centroid $ \\frac{1}{n+1}\\sum_{i=0}^n s_i $ is contained in the affine line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n[5] Affine.Simplex.faceOppositeCentroid_mem_median  (theorem)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.faceOppositeCentroid i ∈ s.median i\n    For an affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over a ring $ k $ in an affine space $ P $, and for each index $ i $ (where $ 0 \\leq i \\leq n $), the centroid of the face opposite to the vertex at index $ i $ is contained in the median of the simplex corresponding to $ i $. The median is defined as the affine line passing through the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $.\n[6] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_faceOppositeCentroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) :\n  dist (t.points i) (t.faceOppositeCentroid i) = 3 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    For any triangle $ t $ in a real affine space and any vertex index $ i $, the distance between the $ i $-th vertex $ t_i $ and the centroid of the opposite face is three times the distance between the centroid of the triangle $ t $ and the centroid of the opposite face. Formally, $ d(t_i, c_{\\text{face}}) = 3 \\cdot d(c_{\\text{triangle}}, c_{\\text{face}}) $, where $ c_{\\text{triangle}} $ denotes the centroid of $ t $ and $ c_{\\text{face}} $ denotes the centroid of the face opposite vertex $ i $.\n[7] Affine.Triangle.dist_div_sin_angle_div_two_eq_circumradius  (theorem)\n    (t : Triangle ℝ P) {i₁ i₂ i₃ : Fin 3} (h₁₂ : i₁ ≠ i₂) (h₁₃ : i₁ ≠ i₃) (h₂₃ : i₂ ≠ i₃) :\n  dist (t.points i₁) (t.points i₃) / Real.sin (∠ (t.points i₁) (t.points i₂) (t.points i₃)) / 2 = t.circumradius\n    For any triangle in a real inner product space, the distance between two vertices divided by twice the sine of the angle at the third vertex equals the circumradius, i.e., $\\frac{\\|p_i - p_k\\|}{2\\sin\\theta_j} = R$, where $\\theta_j$ is the angle at vertex $p_j$ and $R$ is the circumradius.\n[8] Affine.Simplex.point_mem_median  (theorem)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.points i ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ and any index $ i $, the vertex $ s_i $ is contained in the median of $ s $ at index $ i $, which is the affine line connecting $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"median length triangle sides relationship\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.dist_sq_add_dist_sq_eq_two_mul_dist_midpoint_sq_add_half_dist_sq  (theorem)\n    (a b c : P) : dist a b ^ 2 + dist a c ^ 2 = 2 * (dist a (midpoint ℝ b c) ^ 2 + (dist b c / 2) ^ 2)\n    For any points $a$, $b$, and $c$ in an affine space $P$ over the real numbers, the sum of the squares of the distances from $a$ to $b$ and from $a$ to $c$ equals twice the sum of the square of the distance from $a$ to the midpoint of $b$ and $c$ and the square of half the distance between $b$ and $c$. That is, $\\|a - b\\|^2 + \\|a - c\\|^2 = 2\\left(\\|a - m\\|^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\|b - c\\|}{2}\\right)^2\\right)$, where $m$ is the midpoint of $b$ and $c$.\n[2] Affine.Simplex.median  (definition)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : AffineSubspace k P\n    The median of an affine simplex corresponding to the vertex at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through this vertex and the centroid of the face opposite to it. Formally, for a simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $, the median at index $ i $ is the affine subspace defined by the line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n[3] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) : dist (t.points i) t.centroid = 2 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    In an affine triangle over the real numbers, the distance from each vertex to the centroid is twice the distance from the centroid to the midpoint of the opposite side, i.e., $\\text{dist}(v_i, c) = 2 \\cdot \\text{dist}(c, m_i)$ where $c$ is the centroid and $m_i$ is the midpoint of the side opposite vertex $v_i$.\n[4] EuclideanGeometry.dist_lt_of_angle_lt  (theorem)\n    {a b c : P} (h : ¬Collinear ℝ ({ a, b, c } : Set P)) : ∠ a c b < ∠ a b c → dist a b < dist a c\n    In a non-collinear triangle with vertices $a$, $b$, and $c$, if the angle at $c$ between $a$ and $b$ is strictly less than the angle at $b$ between $a$ and $c$ (i.e., $\\angle acb < \\angle abc$), then the distance between $a$ and $b$ is strictly less than the distance between $a$ and $c$ (i.e., $\\operatorname{dist}(a,b) < \\operatorname{dist}(a,c)$).\n[5] Affine.Simplex.median_eq_line_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    [CharZero k] (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.median i = line[k, s.points i, s.centroid]\n    For an affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over an affine space $ P $ with a ring $ k $ of characteristic zero, the median at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $. Formally, $ s.median(i) $ equals the affine line defined by the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the opposite face.\n[6] Affine.Simplex.median.congr_simp  (theorem)\n    ∀ {k : Type u_1} {V : Type u_2} {P : Type u_3} [inst : DivisionRing k] [inst_1 : AddCommGroup V] [inst_2 : Module k V]\n  [inst_3 : AddTorsor V P] {n : ℕ} [inst_4 : NeZero n] (s s_1 : Affine.Simplex k P n),\n  s = s_1 → ∀ (i i_1 : Fin (n + 1)), i = i_1 → s.median i = s_1.median i_1\n    If two affine simplices $ s $ and $ s_1 $ are equal and their indices $ i $ and $ i_1 $ are equal, then the median of $ s $ at index $ i $ is equal to the median of $ s_1 $ at index $ i_1 $. This holds in the context of a division ring $ k $, an additive commutative group $ V $, a $ k $-module $ V $, and an affine torsor $ P $ over $ V $, for a natural number $ n $ (nonzero).\n[7] Affine.Simplex.point_mem_median  (theorem)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.points i ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ and any index $ i $, the vertex $ s_i $ is contained in the median of $ s $ at index $ i $, which is the affine line connecting $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $.\n[8] EuclideanGeometry.angle_le_iff_dist_le  (theorem)\n    {a b c : P} (h : ¬Collinear ℝ ({ a, b, c } : Set P)) : ∠ a c b ≤ ∠ a b c ↔ dist a b ≤ dist a c\n    In a triangle formed by non-collinear points $a$, $b$, and $c$, the angle at $c$ between $a$ and $b$ is less than or equal to the angle at $b$ between $a$ and $c$ if and only if the distance from $a$ to $b$ is less than or equal to the distance from $a$ to $c$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Apollonius theorem median triangle\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"triangle area formula in terms of medians\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] How to find area of triangle from its medians [closed]\n    Closed. This question does not meet Mathematics Stack Exchange guidelines. It is not currently accepting answers. Please provide additional ...\n    https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/396085/how-to-find-area-of-triangle-from-its-medians\n[2] Find The Area Of A Triangle Given The Lengths Of The Medians\n    This video shows how to find the area of a triangle given the length of the medians of the triangle.\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aX9ihZZGquw\n[3] Median (geometry)\n    a median of a triangle is a line. Every triangle has exactly three medians, a = 2 3 − m a. Any triangle's area T can be expressed in terms of its medians m a\n    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Median_(geometry)\n[4] Find the area of triangle whose length of median is 9 cm, ...\n    \"Find the area of triangle whose length of median is 9 cm, 10 cm, and 11 cm?\"\n    https://testbook.com/question-answer/find-the-area-of-triangle-whose-length-of-median-i--5fc1065b2358053aeff2c9a4\n[5] Median of triangle - Formula, Definition, Properties, Examples\n    All triangles have 3 medians (one from each vertex), meeting at the values in the formula, AD = √[(0 - 4)2 + (3 - 10)]2. This can be solved as √(16 + 49) = 8. .