and $c$. That is, $\\|a - b\\|^2 + \\|a - c\\|^2 = 2\\left(\\|a - m\\|^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\|b - c\\|}{2}\\right)^2\\right)$, where $m$ is the midpoint of $b$ and $c$.\n[2] Affine.Simplex.median  (definition)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : AffineSubspace k P\n    The median of an affine simplex corresponding to the vertex at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through this vertex and the centroid of the face opposite to it. Formally, for a simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $, the median at index $ i $ is the affine subspace defined by the line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n[3] Affine.Simplex.median_eq_line_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    [CharZero k] (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.median i = line[k, s.points i, s.centroid]\n    For an affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over an affine space $ P $ with a ring $ k $ of characteristic zero, the median at index $ i $ is the affine line passing through the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $. Formally, $ s.median(i) $ equals the affine line defined by the vertex $ s_i $ and the centroid of the opposite face.\n[4] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_centroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) : dist (t.points i) t.centroid = 2 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    In an affine triangle over the real numbers, the distance from each vertex to the centroid is twice the distance from the centroid to the midpoint of the opposite side, i.e., $\\text{dist}(v_i, c) = 2 \\cdot \\text{dist}(c, m_i)$ where $c$ is the centroid and $m_i$ is the midpoint of the side opposite vertex $v_i$.\n[5] Affine.Triangle.dist_point_faceOppositeCentroid  (theorem)\n    (t : Affine.Triangle ℝ P) (i : Fin 3) :\n  dist (t.points i) (t.faceOppositeCentroid i) = 3 * dist t.centroid (t.faceOppositeCentroid i)\n    For any triangle $ t $ in a real affine space and any vertex index $ i $, the distance between the $ i $-th vertex $ t_i $ and the centroid of the opposite face is three times the distance between the centroid of the triangle $ t $ and the centroid of the opposite face. Formally, $ d(t_i, c_{\\text{face}}) = 3 \\cdot d(c_{\\text{triangle}}, c_{\\text{face}}) $, where $ c_{\\text{triangle}} $ denotes the centroid of $ t $ and $ c_{\\text{face}} $ denotes the centroid of the face opposite vertex $ i $.\n[6] Affine.Simplex.point_mem_median  (theorem)\n    (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.points i ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ and any index $ i $, the vertex $ s_i $ is contained in the median of $ s $ at index $ i $, which is the affine line connecting $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite to $ s_i $.\n[7] Affine.Simplex.median.congr_simp  (theorem)\n    ∀ {k : Type u_1} {V : Type u_2} {P : Type u_3} [inst : DivisionRing k] [inst_1 : AddCommGroup V] [inst_2 : Module k V]\n  [inst_3 : AddTorsor V P] {n : ℕ} [inst_4 : NeZero n] (s s_1 : Affine.Simplex k P n),\n  s = s_1 → ∀ (i i_1 : Fin (n + 1)), i = i_1 → s.median i = s_1.median i_1\n    If two affine simplices $ s $ and $ s_1 $ are equal and their indices $ i $ and $ i_1 $ are equal, then the median of $ s $ at index $ i $ is equal to the median of $ s_1 $ at index $ i_1 $. This holds in the context of a division ring $ k $, an additive commutative group $ V $, a $ k $-module $ V $, and an affine torsor $ P $ over $ V $, for a natural number $ n $ (nonzero).\n[8] Affine.Simplex.centroid_mem_median  (theorem)\n    [CharZero k] (s : Simplex k P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : s.centroid ∈ s.median i\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ of dimension $ n $ over a ring $ k $ with characteristic zero, the centroid of $ s $ lies in the median corresponding to the $ i $-th vertex. Specifically, given a simplex $ s $ with vertices $ s_0, s_1, \\ldots, s_n $, the centroid $ \\frac{1}{n+1}\\sum_{i=0}^n s_i $ is contained in the affine line connecting the vertex $ s_i $ to the centroid of the face opposite $ s_i $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"triangle area from medians formula\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] How to find area of triangle from its medians [closed]\n    Closed. This question does not meet Mathematics Stack Exchange guidelines. It is not currently accepting answers. Please provide additional ...\n    https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/396085/how-to-find-area-of-triangle-from-its-medians\n[2] Find The Area Of A Triangle Given The Lengths Of The Medians\n    This video shows how to find the area of a triangle given the length of the medians of the triangle.\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aX9ihZZGquw\n[3] Area of a Triangle Given the Medians\n    Useful Result: The triangle formed by the medians of a given triangle will have an area three-fourths the area of the given triangle. If ABC is a triangle with ...\n    https://jwilson.coe.uga.edu/emt725/Medians.Triangle/Area.Medians.Tri.html\n[4] Median (geometry)\n    Formulas involving the medians' lengths · a = 2 3 − m a 2 + 2 m b 2 + 2 m c 2 = 2 ( b 2 + c 2 ) − 4 m a 2 = b 2 2 − c 2 + 2 m b 2 = c 2 2 − b 2 + 2 m c 2 · b = 2 ...\n    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Median_(geometry)\n[5] How do I calculate area of triangle from medians?\n    How do I calculate area of triangle from medians?\n    https://www.quora.com/How-do-I-calculate-area-of-triangle-from-medians\n</tool_response>\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Sqrt\n\nopen Real\n\n/--\nGiven a triangle with unequal sides, two medians of lengths 3 and 6, and area 3√15,\nthe third median has length 3√6.\nThe condition of unequal sides implies the medians are pairwise distinct.\n-/\ntheorem triangle_median_length_problem\n  (m1 m2 m3 : ℝ)\n  (h_m1 : m1 = 3)\n  (h_m2 : m2 = 6)\n  (h_m3_pos : m3 > 0)\n  (h_area : 9 * (3 * sqrt 15)^2 = (m1 + m2 + m3) * (m1 + m2 - m3) * (m1 - m2 + m3) * (-m1 + m2 + m3))\n  (h_unequal_sides : m1 ≠ m2 ∧ m2 ≠ m3 ∧ m3 ≠ m1)\n  : m3 = 3 * sqrt 6 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 3 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the length of the third median ($m_3$) of a triangle given two medians ($m_1=3, m_2=6$), the area ($A=3\\sqrt{15}$), and the condition that the triangle has unequal sides (scalene). The mathematical relationship between the area and medians is given by $16 A_{median}^2 = (m_1+m_2+m_3)(m_1+m_2-m_3)(m_1-m_2+m_3)(-m_1+m_2+m_3)$, where $A_{median} = \\frac{3}{4}A$. This simplifies to $9A^2 = \\text{Product of sums/differences}$. The \"unequal sides\" condition implies all medians must be distinct, which is necessary to distinguish between the algebraic solutions $m_3=6$ (isosceles) and $m_3=3\\sqrt{6}$ (scalene).\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem defines real variables `m1, m2, m3`. It fixes `m1=3`, `m2=6`. It asserts `m3 > 0`. It encodes the area constraint as `9 * (3 * sqrt 15)^2 = (m1 + m2 + m3) * ...`, which matches the derived formula $9A^2 = \\text{Product}$. It includes `h_unequal_sides` requiring all medians to be pairwise distinct. The conclusion is `m3 = 3 * sqrt 6`.\n\n3. Comparative Verification: \n   - The area formula in Lean corresponds exactly to the geometric identity relating medians and area.\n   - The values 3, 6, and $3\\sqrt{15}$ are correctly transcribed.\n   - The \"unequal sides\" condition is correctly translated to \"unequal medians\" (`m1 ≠ m2 ∧ m2 ≠ m3 ∧ m3 ≠ m1`), which is mathematically equivalent for triangles and necessary to eliminate the $m_3=6$ solution.\n   - The conclusion `m3 = 3 * sqrt 6` is the unique solution satisfying all constraints.\n   - Types (`ℝ`) and positivity constraints are appropriate.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a precise and mathematically equivalent formalization of the natural language problem. All constraints and data are correctly represented.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The area formula is correct, the specific values are correct, the \"unequal sides\" constraint is correctly interpreted as distinct medians to ensure a unique solution, and the conclusion matches the derived value.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Sqrt\n\nopen Real\n\n/--\nGiven a triangle with unequal sides, two medians of lengths 3 and 6, and area 3√15,\nthe third median has length 3√6.\nThe condition of unequal sides implies the medians are pairwise distinct.\n-/\ntheorem triangle_median_length_problem\n  (m1 m2 m3 : ℝ)\n  (h_m1 : m1 = 3)\n  (h_m2 : m2 = 6)\n  (h_m3_pos : m3 > 0)\n  (h_area : 9 * (3 * sqrt 15)^2 = (m1 + m2 + m3) * (m1 + m2 - m3) * (m1 - m2 + m3) * (-m1 + m2 + m3))\n  (h_unequal_sides : m1 ≠ m2 ∧ m2 ≠ m3 ∧ m3 ≠ m1)\n  : m3 = 3 * sqrt 6 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Two medians of a triangle with unequal sides are $3$ inches and $6$ inches. Its area is $3 \\sqrt{15}$ square inches. The length of the third median in inches, is:\n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 4\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 3\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 3\\sqrt{6}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 6\\sqrt{6}$", "num_rounds": 3, "num_tool_calls": 8, "tools_used": ["leansearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_amc_aime", "question_id": "amc_aime_198626"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nSuppose that $V$ is finite dimensional and that $T \\in \\mathcal{L}(V, W)$. Prove that there exists a subspace $U$ of $V$ such that $U \\cap \\operatorname{null} T=\\{0\\}$ and range $T=\\{T u: u \\in U\\}$."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"linear map kernel null space range image finite dimensional vector space\", \"num_results\": 10}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] LinearMap.finrank_range_add_finrank_ker  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] (f : V →ₗ[K] V₂) : finrank K (LinearMap.range f) + finrank K (LinearMap.ker f) = finrank K V\n    For any linear map $ f : V \\to V_2 $ where $ V $ is a finite-dimensional vector space over a division ring $ K $, the sum of the dimension of the image of $ f $ and the dimension of the kernel of $ f $ equals the dimension of $ V $. In symbols, $ \\dim(\\text{im}(f)) + \\dim(\\ker(f)) = \\dim(V) $.\n[2] LinearMap.ker_eq_bot_iff_range_eq_top  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] {f : V →ₗ[K] V} : LinearMap.ker f = ⊥ ↔ LinearMap.range f = ⊤\n    For a finite-dimensional vector space $ V $ over a division ring $ K $ and a linear map $ f : V \\to V $, the kernel of $ f $ is trivial (i.e., $ \\ker(f) = \\{0\\} $) if and only if the range of $ f $ is the entire space $ V $ (i.e., $ \\mathrm{range}(f) = V $).\n[3] LinearMap.ker  (definition)\n    (f : M →ₛₗ[τ₁₂] M₂) : Submodule R M\n    The kernel of a linear map $ f : M → M_2 $ is the submodule of $ M $ consisting of all elements $ x $ such that $ f(x) = 0 $ in $ M_2 $. It is defined as the preimage of the trivial submodule $ \\{0\\} $ under $ f $.\n[4] LinearMap.ker_ne_bot_of_finrank_lt  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] [FiniteDimensional K V₂] {f : V →ₗ[K] V₂} (h : finrank K V₂ < finrank K V) :\n  LinearMap.ker f ≠ ⊥\n    For finite-dimensional vector spaces $V$ and $V_2$ over a division ring $K$, if the dimension of $V_2$ is strictly less than the dimension of $V$, then any linear map $f : V \\to V_2$ has a non-trivial kernel, i.e., $\\ker(f) \\neq \\{0\\}$.\n[5] LinearMap.ker_eq_bot_iff_range_eq_top_of_finrank_eq_finrank  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] [FiniteDimensional K V₂] (H : finrank K V = finrank K V₂) {f : V →ₗ[K] V₂} :\n  LinearMap.ker f = ⊥ ↔ LinearMap.range f = ⊤\n    For finite-dimensional vector spaces $ V $ and $ V_2 $ over a division ring $ K $ with equal finite ranks, a linear map $ f : V \\to V_2 $ has a trivial kernel ($ \\ker(f) = \\{0\\} $) if and only if its range is the entire space $ V_2 $ ($ \\mathrm{range}(f) = V_2 $).\n[6] LinearMap.isUnit_iff_ker_eq_bot  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] (f : V →ₗ[K] V) : IsUnit f ↔ (LinearMap.ker f) = ⊥\n    For a finite-dimensional vector space $ V $ over a division ring $ K $, a linear map $ f : V \\to V $ is invertible if and only if its kernel is trivial, i.e., $ \\ker(f) = \\{0\\} $.\n[7] ContinuousLinearMap.ker_le_ker_iff_range_le_range  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional 𝕜 E] {T U : E →L[𝕜] E} (hT : T.IsSymmetric) (hU : U.IsSymmetric) : U.ker ≤ T.ker ↔ T.range ≤ U.range\n    For symmetric linear maps $T$ and $U$ on a finite-dimensional vector space $E$ over a field $\\mathbb{K}$, the kernel of $U$ is contained in the kernel of $T$ if and only if the range of $T$ is contained in the range of $U$.\n[8] LinearMap.ker_rangeRestrict  (theorem)\n    : ker f.rangeRestrict = ker f\n    For a linear map $ f $, the kernel of the restriction of $ f $ to its range is equal to the kernel of $ f $ itself, i.e., $\\ker(f|_{\\operatorname{range}(f)}) = \\ker(f)$.\n[9] DivisionRing.hasRankNullity  (instance)\n    : HasRankNullity.{u₀} K\n    Every division ring satisfies the rank-nullity theorem, which states that for any linear map between finite-dimensional vector spaces over the division ring, the dimension of the domain equals the sum of the dimensions of the kernel and the image.\n[10] ContinuousLinearMap.ker_closedComplemented_of_finiteDimensional_range  (theorem)\n    (f : E →L[𝕜] F) [FiniteDimensional 𝕜 f.range] : f.ker.ClosedComplemented\n    Let $ \\mathbb{K} $ be a normed field, and let $ E $, $ F $ be normed modules over $ \\mathbb{K} $. For any continuous linear map $ f : E \\to F $, if the image $ \\mathrm{range}(f) $ is finite-dimensional, then the kernel $ \\ker(f) $ is a closed complemented submodule of $ E $. This means there exists a continuous linear map $ r : E \\to \\ker(f) $ such that $ r(x) = x $ for all $ x \\in \\ker(f) $, making $ \\ker(f) $ a direct summand of $ E $ in the category of topological modules.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"subspace complement direct sum kernel range linear map exists subspace\", \"num_results\": 10}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] ContinuousLinearMap.range_eq_map_coprodSubtypeLEquivOfIsCompl  (theorem)\n    {F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace 𝕜 F] [CompleteSpace F] (f : E →L[𝕜] F) {G : Submodule 𝕜 F}\n  (h : IsCompl f.range G) [CompleteSpace G] (hker : f.ker = ⊥) :\n  f.range = ((⊤ : Submodule 𝕜 E).prod (⊥ : Submodule 𝕜 G)).map (f.coprodSubtypeLEquivOfIsCompl h hker : E × G →ₗ[𝕜] F)\n    Let $ E $ and $ F $ be normed vector spaces over a field $ \\mathbb{K} $, with $ F $ complete. For a continuous linear map $ f : E \\to F $ with trivial kernel ($ \\ker f = \\{0\\} $), and a closed subspace $ G \\subseteq F $ complementary to $ \\mathrm{range}\\, f $ (i.e., $ \\mathrm{range}\\, f \\oplus G = F $), the range of $ f $ coincides with the image of the product $ E \\times \\{0\\} $ under the canonical linear equivalence $ E \\times G \\xrightarrow{\\cong} F $ induced by the direct sum decomposition. This equivalence identifies $ E $ with $ \\mathrm{range}\\, f $ via the isomorphism $ (v, g) \\mapsto f...\n[2] LinearMap.kerComplementEquivRange  (definition)\n    {R M M₂ : Type*} [Ring R] [AddCommGroup M] [AddCommGroup M₂] [Module R M] [Module R M₂] (f : M →ₗ[R] M₂)\n  {C : Submodule R M} (h : IsCompl C (LinearMap.ker f)) : C ≃ₗ[R] range f\n    Let $f: M \\to M_2$ be a linear map between modules over a ring $R$. If $C$ is a submodule of $M$ that is a complement to the kernel of $f$ (i.e., $M = C \\oplus \\ker f$), then $C$ is linearly isomorphic to the range of $f$.\n[3] LinearMap.IsIdempotentElem.isCompl  (theorem)\n    {f : E →ₗ[R] E} (hf : IsIdempotentElem f) : IsCompl (range f) (ker f)\n    For an idempotent linear map $ f : E o E $, the range of $ f $ and the kernel of $ f $ are complementary submodules of $ E $.\n[4] ContinuousLinearMap.closed_complemented_range_of_isCompl_of_ker_eq_bot  (theorem)\n    {F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace 𝕜 F] [CompleteSpace F] (f : E →L[𝕜] F) (G : Submodule 𝕜 F)\n  (h : IsCompl f.range G) (hG : IsClosed (G : Set F)) (hker : f.ker = ⊥) : IsClosed (f.range : Set F)\n    Let $ E $ and $ F $ be normed vector spaces over a field $ \\mathbb{K} $, with $ F $ complete. If $ f : E \\to F $ is a continuous linear map with trivial kernel ($ \\ker f = \\{0\\} $), and there exists a closed submodule $ G \\subseteq F $ such that $ G $ is a complement of $ \\operatorname{range} f $ (i.e., $ F = \\operatorname{range} f \\oplus G $), then the image $ \\operatorname{range} f $ is a closed subspace of $ F $.\n[5] LinearMap.comap_eq_sup_ker_of_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {p : Submodule K V} [FiniteDimensional K p] {f : V →ₗ[K] V} (h : ∀ x ∈ p, f x ∈ p) (h' : Disjoint p (ker f)) :\n  p.comap f = p ⊔ ker f\n    For a finite-dimensional submodule $ p $ of a vector space $ V $ over a division ring $ K $, and a linear map $ f : V \\to V $ such that $ f(p) \\subseteq p $ and $ p $ is disjoint from the kernel of $ f $, the preimage of $ p $ under $ f $ is equal to the sum of $ p $ and the kernel of $ f $, i.e., $ f^{-1}(p) = p + \\ker f $.\n[6] LinearMap.range_ofIsCompl  (theorem)\n    (hpq : IsCompl p q) {φ : p →ₗ[R] F} {ψ : q →ₗ[R] F} : range (ofIsCompl hpq φ ψ) = range φ ⊔ range ψ\n    Given complementary submodules $ p $ and $ q $ of a module $ E $ over a semiring $ R $, and linear maps $ \\varphi: p \\to F $ and $ \\psi: q \\to F $, the range of the linear map $ E \\to F $ constructed by combining $ \\varphi $ and $ \\psi $ is equal to the sum of the ranges of $ \\varphi $ and $ \\psi $.\n[7] LinearMap.isCompl_of_proj  (theorem)\n    {f : E →ₗ[R] p} (hf : ∀ x : p, f x = x) : IsCompl p (ker f)\n    Let $ E $ be an $ R $-module and $ p $ a submodule of $ E $. If $ f : E \\to p $ is a linear map satisfying $ f(x) = x $ for all $ x \\in p $, then $ p $ and the kernel of $ f $ are complementary submodules of $ E $. This means $ p \\cap \\ker f = \\{0\\} $ and $ p + \\ker f = E $.\n[8] LinearMap.isCompl_iSup_ker_pow_iInf_range_pow  (theorem)\n    [IsNoetherian R M] (f : M →ₗ[R] M) : IsCompl (⨆ n, LinearMap.ker (f ^ n)) (⨅ n, LinearMap.range (f ^ n))\n    Let $ M $ be a Noetherian module over a semiring $ R $, and let $ f : M \\to M $ be a linear map. The supremum of the kernels $ \\ker(f^n) $ for all $ n \\geq 1 $ and the infimum of the ranges $ \\operatorname{range}(f^n) $ for all $ n \\geq 1 $ form complementary submodules of $ M $. This means their intersection is the trivial submodule $ \\{0\\} $ and their sum equals $ M $.\n[9] LinearMap.span_singleton_sup_ker_eq_top  (theorem)\n    (f : V →ₗ[K] K) {x : V} (hx : f x ≠ 0) : K ∙ x ⊔ ker f = ⊤\n    For a linear map $ f : V \\to K $ from a vector space $ V $ over a field $ K $, and any $ x \\in V $ with $ f(x) \\neq 0 $, the sum of the span of $ x $ and the kernel of $ f $ equals the entire space $ V $. That is, $ \\langle x \\rangle + \\ker(f) = V $.\n[10] Submodule.linearProjOfIsCompl_ker  (theorem)\n    (h : IsCompl p q) : ker (linearProjOfIsCompl p q h) = q\n    For complementary submodules $ p $ and $ q $ of a module $ E $ over a semiring $ R $, the kernel of the linear projection map from $ E $ to $ p $ constructed via their complementarity is precisely the submodule $ q $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"exists subspace complement kernel finite dimensional vector space\", \"num_results\": 10}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Submodule.ClosedComplemented.of_quotient_finiteDimensional  (theorem)\n    [CompleteSpace 𝕜] [FiniteDimensional 𝕜 (E ⧸ p)] (hp : IsClosed (p : Set E)) : p.ClosedComplemented\n    Let $ E $ be a normed vector space over a complete field $ \\mathbb{K} $, and let $ p $ be a closed submodule of $ E $. If the quotient space $ E/p $ is finite-dimensional over $ \\mathbb{K} $, then $ p $ is closed complemented, i.e., there exists a continuous linear map $ f : E \\to p $ such that $ f(x) = x $ for all $ x \\in p $.\n[2] Submodule.finrank_add_eq_of_isCompl  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] {U W : Submodule K V} (h : IsCompl U W) : finrank K U + finrank K W = finrank K V\n    For a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ over a division ring $K$, if $U$ and $W$ are complementary submodules of $V$ (i.e., $U + W = V$ and $U \\cap W = \\{0\\}$), then the sum of their finite ranks satisfies $\\dim(U) + \\dim(W) = \\dim(V)$.\n[3] Module.Dual.isCompl_ker_of_disjoint_of_ne_bot  (theorem)\n    {p : Submodule K V₁} (hpf : Disjoint (LinearMap.ker f) p) (hp : p ≠ ⊥) : IsCompl (LinearMap.ker f) p\n    Let $ f $ be a linear map between modules over a division ring $ K $. If the kernel of $ f $ and a submodule $ p $ are disjoint (i.e., their intersection contains only the zero element) and $ p $ is not the trivial submodule $ \\{0\\} $, then the kernel of $ f $ and $ p $ form a complementary pair in the lattice of submodules. This means their sum equals the entire module and their intersection is trivial.\n[4] LinearMap.span_singleton_sup_ker_eq_top  (theorem)\n    (f : V →ₗ[K] K) {x : V} (hx : f x ≠ 0) : K ∙ x ⊔ ker f = ⊤\n    For a linear map $ f : V \\to K $ from a vector space $ V $ over a field $ K $, and any $ x \\in V $ with $ f(x) \\neq 0 $, the sum of the span of $ x $ and the kernel of $ f $ equals the entire space $ V $. That is, $ \\langle x \\rangle + \\ker(f) = V $.\n[5] Submodule.isCompl_iff_disjoint  (theorem)\n    [FiniteDimensional K V] (s t : Submodule K V) (hdim : finrank K V ≤ finrank K s + finrank K t) :\n  IsCompl s t ↔ Disjoint s t\n    In a finite-dimensional vector space $ V $ over a division ring $ K $, for submodules $ s $ and $ t $, if the dimension of $ V $ is at most the sum of the dimensions of $ s $ and $ t $, then $ s $ and $ t $ are complementary submodules (i.e., their direct sum equals $ V $) if and only if they are disjoint (i.e., their intersection is the trivial submodule $\\{0\\}$).\n[6] Submodule.exists_isCompl  (theorem)\n    (p : Submodule K V) : ∃ q : Submodule K V, IsCompl p q\n    For any submodule $ p $ of a vector space $ V $ over a division ring $ K $, there exists a submodule $ q $ such that $ p $ and $ q $ are complementary. This means that the intersection of $ p $ and $ q $ is the trivial submodule $ \\{0\\} $, and their sum $ p + q $ equals the entire space $ V $.\n[7] Submodule.linearProjOfIsCompl_ker  (theorem)\n    (h : IsCompl p q) : ker (linearProjOfIsCompl p q h) = q\n    For complementary submodules $ p $ and $ q $ of a module $ E $ over a semiring $ R $, the kernel of the linear projection map from $ E $ to $ p $ constructed via their complementarity is precisely the submodule $ q $.\n[8] ContinuousLinearMap.ker_closedComplemented_of_finiteDimensional_range  (theorem)\n    (f : E →L[𝕜] F) [FiniteDimensional 𝕜 f.range] : f.ker.ClosedComplemented\n    Let $ \\mathbb{K} $ be a normed field, and let $ E $, $ F $ be normed modules over $ \\mathbb{K} $. For any continuous linear map $ f : E \\to F $, if the image $ \\mathrm{range}(f) $ is finite-dimensional, then the kernel $ \\ker(f) $ is a closed complemented submodule of $ E $. This means there exists a continuous linear map $ r : E \\to \\ker(f) $ such that $ r(x) = x $ for all $ x \\in \\ker(f) $, making $ \\ker(f) $ a direct summand of $ E $ in the category of topological modules.\n[9] LinearMap.IsProj.isCompl  (theorem)\n    {f : E →ₗ[R] E} (h : IsProj p f) : IsCompl p (ker f)\n    If $ f : E \\to E $ is a linear projection onto a submodule $ p $, then $ p $ and the kernel of $ f $ form complementary submodules, i.e., $ p \\cap \\ker f = \\{0\\} $ and $ p + \\ker f = E $.\n[10] LinearMap.comap_eq_sup_ker_of_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {p : Submodule K V} [FiniteDimensional K p] {f : V →ₗ[K] V} (h : ∀ x ∈ p, f x ∈ p) (h' : Disjoint p (ker f)) :\n  p.comap f = p ⊔ ker f\n    For a finite-dimensional submodule $ p $ of a vector space $ V $ over a division ring $ K $, and a linear map $ f : V \\to V $ such that $ f(p) \\subseteq p $ and $ p $ is disjoint from the kernel of $ f $, the preimage of $ p $ under $ f $ is equal to the sum of $ p $ and the kernel of $ f $, i.e., $ f^{-1}(p) = p + \\ker f $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize this theorem. Let me analyze:\n\n1. The problem asks to prove that for a linear map T from V to W where V is finite dimensional, there exists a subspace U of V such that:\n   - U ∩ null T = {0} (U and kernel of T are disjoint)\n   - range T = {Tu : u ∈ U} (the image of U under T equals the range of T)\n\n2. From the search results, I can see:\n   - `LinearMap.ker` is the kernel/null space\n   - `LinearMap.range` is the range/image\n   - `Submodule.exists_isCompl` states that any submodule has a complement\n   - `LinearMap.kerComplementEquivRange` shows that a complement of the kernel is isomorphic to the range\n\n3. The key insight is that if U is a complement of ker T, then:\n   - U ∩ ker T = {0} (by definition of complement, which uses `Disjoint`)\n   - The restriction of T to U maps U onto range T\n\nLet me write the formal statement:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nvariable {K V W : Type*} [Field K] [AddCommGroup V] [Module K V] [AddCommGroup W] [Module K W]\n\n/-- For a linear map T from a finite-dimensional vector space V to W, there exists a subspace U of V \nsuch that U intersects the kernel of T trivially and the image of U under T equals the range of T. -/\ntheorem exists_subspace_complement_kernel (T : V →ₗ[K] W) [FiniteDimensional K V] :\n  ∃ U : Submodule K V, Disjoint U (LinearMap.ker T) ∧ LinearMap.range T = LinearMap.range (T.restrict U) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: Application type mismatch: The argument\n  U\nhas type\n  Submodule K V\nof sort `Type u_2` but is expected to have type\n  ∀ x ∈ ?m.67, T x ∈ ?m.68\nof sort `Prop` in the application\n  T.restrict U\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"LinearMap restrict submodule domain\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] LinearMap.domRestrict_apply  (theorem)\n    (f : M →ₛₗ[σ₁₂] M₂) (p : Submodule R M) (x : p) : f.domRestrict p x = f x\n    Given an $ R $-linear map $ f : M o M_2 $ and a submodule $ p $ of $ M $, the domain restriction of $ f $ to $ p $ is the linear map obtained by composing $ f $ with the canonical inclusion of $ p $ into $ M $, resulting in a map $ p o M_2 $ that preserves addition and scalar multiplication.\n[2] LinearMap.domRestrict'_apply  (theorem)\n    (f : M →ₗ[R] M₂) (p : Submodule R M) (x : p) : domRestrict' p f x = f x\n    For a submodule $ p $ of an $ R $-module $ M $, the function that maps each $ R $-linear map $ f : M \\to M_2 $ to its restriction $ f|_p : p \\to M_2 $ is itself a linear map. This operation preserves pointwise addition and scalar multiplication of linear maps, making the restriction map a linear transformation between the spaces of linear maps.\n[3] LinearMap.toPMap_domain  (theorem)\n    (f : E →ₗ[R] F) (p : Submodule R E) : (f.toPMap p).domain = p\n    For a linear map $ f : E \\to F $ over a ring $ R $ and a submodule $ p \\subseteq E $, the domain of the partial linear map obtained by restricting $ f $ to $ p $ is equal to $ p $ itself, i.e., $ \\operatorname{domain}(f|_p) = p $.\n[4] LinearMap.domRestrict  (definition)\n    (f : M →ₛₗ[σ₁₂] M₂) (p : Submodule R M) : p →ₛₗ[σ₁₂] M₂\n    Given an $ R $-linear map $ f : M \\to M_2 $ and a submodule $ p $ of $ M $, the domain restriction of $ f $ to $ p $ is the linear map obtained by composing $ f $ with the canonical inclusion of $ p $ into $ M $, resulting in a map $ p \\to M_2 $ that preserves addition and scalar multiplication.\n[5] LinearMap.coe_domRestrict  (theorem)\n    (f : M →ₛₗ[σ₁₂] M₂) (p : Submodule R M) : ⇑(f.domRestrict p) = Set.restrict p f\n    For an $ R $-linear map $ f : M \\to M_2 $ and a submodule $ p $ of $ M $, the canonical function obtained by restricting the domain of $ f $ to $ p $ is equal to the restriction of $ f $ to $ p $ as a set.\n[6] LinearPMap.domRestrict_domain  (theorem)\n    (f : E →ₗ.[R] F) {S : Submodule R E} : (f.domRestrict S).domain = S ⊓ f.domain\n    The domain of the restriction of an $ R $-linear map $ f : E \\to F $ to a submodule $ S \\subseteq E $ is equal to the intersection of $ S $ and the original domain of $ f $, i.e., $ \\mathrm{domain}(f|_S) = S \\cap \\mathrm{domain}(f) $.\n[7] LinearPMap.domRestrict  (definition)\n    (f : E →ₗ.[R] F) (S : Submodule R E) : E →ₗ.[R] F\n    Given an $ R $-linear map $ f : E o F $ and a submodule $ S $ of $ E $, this constructs a new $ R $-linear map whose domain is restricted to $ S $. The resulting map is defined by composing $ f $ with the canonical inclusion of $ S $ into $ E $, effectively restricting $ f $ to act only on elements of $ S $.\n[8] LinearMap.restrict  (definition)\n    (f : M →ₗ[R] M₁) {p : Submodule R M} {q : Submodule R M₁} (hf : ∀ x ∈ p, f x ∈ q) : p →ₗ[R] q\n    Given an $R$-linear map $f: M \\to M_1$, a submodule $p$ of $M$, and a submodule $q$ of $M_1$ such that the image of $p$ under $f$ is contained in $q$ (i.e., for all $x \\in p$, $f(x) \\in q$), this defines the restriction of $f$ as a linear map from $p$ to $q$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Submodule Disjoint intersection zero\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Submodule.disjoint_def  (theorem)\n    {p p' : Submodule R M} : Disjoint p p' ↔ ∀ x ∈ p, x ∈ p' → x = (0 : M)\n    For any semiring $ R $ and $ R $-module $ M $, two submodules $ p $ and $ p' $ are disjoint if and only if every element common to both $ p $ and $ p' $ is the zero element of $ M $.\n[2] Submodule.mem_left_iff_eq_zero_of_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {p p' : Submodule R M} (h : Disjoint p p') {x : p'} : (x : M) ∈ p ↔ x = 0\n    If two submodules $ p $ and $ p' $ of an $ R $-module $ M $ are disjoint, then any element $ x \\in p' $ that also belongs to $ p $ must satisfy $ x = 0 $. This characterizes the trivial intersection property of disjoint submodules.\n[3] Submodule.mem_right_iff_eq_zero_of_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {p p' : Submodule R M} (h : Disjoint p p') {x : p} : (x : M) ∈ p' ↔ x = 0\n    Let $ p $ and $ p' $ be disjoint submodules of a module $ M $ over a semiring $ R $. For any element $ x $ in $ p $, $ x $ belongs to $ p' $ if and only if $ x = 0 $.\n[4] Submodule.LinearDisjoint.bot_left  (theorem)\n    : (⊥ : Submodule R S).LinearDisjoint N\n    The trivial submodule $\\{0\\}$ and any submodule $N$ of an $R$-algebra $S$ are linearly disjoint, meaning their intersection contains only the zero element.\n[5] Submodule.LinearDisjoint.bot_right  (theorem)\n    : M.LinearDisjoint (⊥ : Submodule R S)\n    For any submodule $M$ of an $R$-algebra $S$, the submodule $M$ is linearly disjoint with the trivial submodule $\\{0\\}$, meaning their intersection contains only the zero element.\n</tool_response>\nLooking at the error, the issue is that `T.restrict U` requires a proof that