gle $ t $ with distinct vertices $ p_1, p_2, p_3 $, the ratio of the distance between $ p_1 $ and $ p_3 $ to the sine of the angle at $ p_2 $ formed by $ p_1, p_2, p_3 $ is equal to twice the circumradius of the triangle. Formally, $ \\frac{d(p_1, p_3)}{\\sin(\\angle p_1 p_2 p_3)} = 2R $, where $ R $ denotes the circumradius.\n[5] Affine.Triangle.circumsphere_eq_of_dist_of_oangle  (theorem)\n    (t : Triangle ℝ P) {i₁ i₂ i₃ : Fin 3} (h₁₂ : i₁ ≠ i₂) (h₁₃ : i₁ ≠ i₃) (h₂₃ : i₂ ≠ i₃) :\n  t.circumsphere =\n    ⟨((Real.Angle.tan (∡ (t.points i₁) (t.points i₂) (t.points i₃)))⁻¹ / 2) •\n          «o».rotation (π / 2 : ℝ) (t.points i₃ -ᵥ t.points i₁) +ᵥ\n        midpoint ℝ (t.points i₁) (t.points i₃),\n      dist (t.points i₁) (t.points i₃) / |Real.Angle.sin (∡ (t.points i₁) (t.points i₂) (t.points i₃))| / 2⟩\n    For any triangle $ t $ in a real affine space $ P $ with distinct vertices indexed by $ i_1, i_2, i_3 $, the circumsphere of $ t $ has center given by $\\frac{1}{2} \\cot(\\angle(t_{i_1}, t_{i_2}, t_{i_3})) \\cdot R_{\\pi/2}(t_{i_3} - t_{i_1}) + \\text{midpoint}(t_{i_1}, t_{i_3})$ and radius $\\frac{\\|t_{i_3} - t_{i_1}\\|}{2|\\sin(\\angle(t_{i_1}, t_{i_2}, t_{i_3}))|}$, where $ R_{\\pi/2} $ denotes rotation by $ \\pi/2 $ radians.\n[6] Affine.Simplex.dist_circumcenter_eq_circumradius  (theorem)\n    {n : ℕ} (s : Simplex ℝ P n) (i : Fin (n + 1)) : dist (s.points i) s.circumcenter = s.circumradius\n    For any simplex $ s $ in a real inner product space and for any vertex $ v_i $ of $ s $, the distance between $ v_i $ and the circumcenter $ c $ of $ s $ is equal to the circumradius $ r $ of $ s $, i.e., $ \\text{dist}(v_i, c) = r $.\n[7] Affine.Simplex.dist_circumcenter_eq_circumradius'  (theorem)\n    {n : ℕ} (s : Simplex ℝ P n) : ∀ i, dist s.circumcenter (s.points i) = s.circumradius\n    For any affine simplex $ s $ in a real inner product space, the distance from the circumcenter of $ s $ to each of its vertices is equal to the circumradius of $ s $. That is, fo