{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n/-- Prove that, for every set $X = \\{x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n\\}$ of $n$ real numbers, there exists a non-empty subset $S$ of $X$ and an integer $m$ such that\n\\[\n\\left| m + \\sum_{s \\in S} s \\right| \\leq \\frac{1}{n+1}.\n\\]-/"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"finite set sum over subset Finset\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] finsum_mem_eq_toFinset_sum  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} {M : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] (f : α → M) (s : Set α) [inst_1 : Fintype ↑s],\n  ∑ᶠ (i : α) (_ : i ∈ s), f i = ∑ i ∈ s.toFinset, f i\n    For any function $ f : \\alpha \\to M $ where $ M $ is an additive commutative monoid, and for any finite set $ s \\subseteq \\alpha $, the finsum of $ f $ over $ s $ is equal to the sum of $ f $ over the corresponding Finset $ s.toFinset $. This equality holds under the assumption that $ \\alpha $ is equipped with a finite type instance for $ s $.\n[2] Finset.sum_set_coe  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_4} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {f : ι → M} (s : Set ι) [inst_1 : Fintype ↑s],\n  ∑ i, f ↑i = ∑ i ∈ s.toFinset, f i\n    For any additive commutative monoid $ M $, type $ \\iota $, function $ f : \\iota \\to M $, and finite set $ s \\subseteq \\iota $, the sum of $ f(i) $ over all $ i \\in s $ is equal to the sum of $ f(i) $ over the Finset representation of $ s $. Formally, $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) = \\sum_{i \\in s.\\text{toFinset}} f(i) $.\n[3] Finset.subsetSum  (definition)\n    (A : Finset M) : Finset M\n    The finite set consisting of the sum of elements of each subset of $ A $, where the sum is computed in the additive commutative monoid $ M $. Formally, it is the image of the function that maps each subset $ B \\subseteq A $ to the sum of its elements $ \\sum_{b \\in B} b $.\n[4] finsum_eq_sum_of_support_toFinset_subset  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} {M : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] (f : α → M) (hf : (Function.support f).Finite) {s : Finset α},\n  hf.toFinset ⊆ s → ∑ᶠ (i : α), f i = ∑ i ∈ s, f i\n    For a function $ f : \\alpha \\to M $ with finite support in an additive commutative monoid $ M $, if the Finset corresponding to the support of $ f $ is a subset of a Finset $ s $, then the finite sum $ \\sum^\\ast_{i} f(i) $ equals the sum $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) $.\n[5] finsum_mem_eq_finite_toFinset_sum  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} {M : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] (f : α → M) {s : Set α} (hs : s.Finite),\n  ∑ᶠ (i : α) (_ : i ∈ s), f i = ∑ i ∈ hs.toFinset, f i\n    For any function $ f : \\alpha \\to M $ where $ M $ is an additive commutative monoid, and for any finite set $ s \\subseteq \\alpha $, the finite sum $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) $ is equal to the sum over the Finset $ s $, i.e., $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) = \\sum_{i \\in \\text{toFinset}(s)} f(i) $. Here, $ \\text{toFinset}(s) $ denotes the conversion of the finite set $ s $ to a Finset structure.\n[6] finsum_eq_sum_plift_of_support_toFinset_subset  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} {α : Sort u_4} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {f : α → M} (hf : (Function.support (f ∘ PLift.down)).Finite)\n  {s : Finset (PLift α)}, hf.toFinset ⊆ s → ∑ᶠ (i : α), f i = ∑ i ∈ s, f i.down\n    Let $ M $ be an additive commutative monoid and $ \\alpha $ a type. For any function $ f : \\alpha \\to M $ with finite support, if the support of $ f $ (converted to a finite set) is a subset of a finite set $ s $, then the sum of $ f $ over all $ \\alpha $ (denoted $ \\sum^\\infty f(i) $) equals the sum of $ f $ over the elements of $ s $, i.e., $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) $.\n[7] Fintype.sum_ite_mem  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_3} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] [inst_1 : Fintype ι] [inst_2 : DecidableEq ι] (s : Finset ι)\n  (f : ι → M), (∑ i, if i ∈ s then f i else 0) = ∑ i ∈ s, f i\n    For any finite type $\\iota$ and additive commutative monoid $M$, given a finite set $s \\subseteq \\iota$ and a function $f : \\iota \\to M$, the sum of $f(i)$ over all $i \\in \\iota$ with an indicator condition (adding $f(i)$ if $i \\in s$ and 0 otherwise) equals the sum of $f(i)$ over all $i \\in s$. Formally, $\\sum_{i \\in \\iota} (\\text{if } i \\in s \\text{ then } f(i) \\text{ else } 0) = \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i)$.\n[8] Set.finset_sum_subset_finset_sum  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {α : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid α] (t : Finset ι) (f₁ f₂ : ι → Set α),\n  (∀ i ∈ t, f₁ i ⊆ f₂ i) → ∑ i ∈ t, f₁ i ⊆ ∑ i ∈ t, f₂ i\n    Let $\\alpha$ be an additive commutative monoid, $\\iota$ an index type, and $t$ a finite set of $\\iota$. For functions $f_1, f_2 : \\iota \\to \\mathcal{P}(\\alpha)$ (where $\\mathcal{P}(\\alpha)$ denotes the power set of $\\alpha$), if $f_1(i) \\subseteq f_2(i)$ for all $i \\in t$, then the Minkowski sum $\\sum_{i \\in t} f_1(i)$ is a subset of $\\sum_{i \\in t} f_2(i)$. The Minkowski sum of a family of sets is defined as $\\sum_{i \\in t} S_i = \\{s_1 + \\cdots + s_n \\mid s_i \\in S_i\\}$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"abs real absolute value inequality\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.le_norm_self  (theorem)\n    (r : ℝ) : r ≤ ‖r‖\n    For any real number $ r $, it holds that $ r \\leq |r| $.\n[2] Real.abs_le_sqrt  (theorem)\n    (h : x ^ 2 ≤ y) : |x| ≤ √y\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $y$, if $x^2 \\leq y$, then $|x| \\leq \\sqrt{y}$.\n[3] abs_le_abs  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : Lattice α] [inst_1 : AddGroup α] {a b : α}, a ≤ b → -a ≤ b → |a| ≤ |b|\n    For any elements $ a $ and $ b $ in a type $ \\alpha $ equipped with both an additive group structure and a lattice structure, if $ a \\leq b $ and $ -a \\leq b $, then the absolute value of $ a $, defined as $ \\sup(a, -a) $, satisfies $ |a| \\leq |b| $, where $ |b| $ is similarly defined as $ \\sup(b, -b) $.\n[4] Real.nnabs_pos  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} : 0 < x.nnabs ↔ x ≠ 0\n    For any real number $x$, $0 < |x|$ if and only if $x eq 0$.\n[5] abs_le  (theorem)\n    ∀ {G : Type u_1} [inst : AddCommGroup G] [inst_1 : LinearOrder G] [IsOrderedAddMonoid G] {a b : G},\n  |a| ≤ b ↔ -b ≤ a ∧ a ≤ b\n    In an additive commutative group equipped with a linear order and compatible addition (i.e., an ordered additive commutative monoid), the absolute value of an element $a$ (defined as $\\sup(a, -a)$) satisfies $|a| \\leq b$ if and only if $-b \\leq a \\leq b$.\n[6] abs_le'  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : Lattice α] [inst_1 : AddGroup α] {a b : α}, |a| ≤ b ↔ a ≤ b ∧ -a ≤ b\n    For any elements $a$ and $b$ in a type $\\alpha$ equipped with an additive group and a lattice structure, the absolute value of $a$, defined as the supremum of $a$ and $-a$, satisfies $|a| \\leq b$ if and only if both $a \\leq b$ and $-a \\leq b$.\n[7] EReal.abs_coe_lt_top  (theorem)\n    (x : ℝ) : (x : EReal).abs < ⊤\n    For any real number $ x $, the absolute value of $ x $ in the extended non-negative real numbers is less than $ \\infty $.\n[8] abs_lt_of_sq_lt_sq  (theorem)\n    (h : a ^ 2 < b ^ 2) (hb : 0 ≤ b) : |a| < b\n    If $ a^2 < b^2 $ and $ 0 \\leq b $, then $ |a| < b $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize the statement. Let me write the Lean 4 theorem:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real Finset\n\n/-- For every finite set X of n real numbers, there exists a non-empty subset S of X \n    and an integer m such that |m + sum of elements in S| ≤ 1/(n+1). -/\ntheorem subset_sum_approximation (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) (X : Finset ℝ) (hX : X.card = n) :\n  ∃ (S : Finset ℝ) (m : ℤ), S ⊆ X ∧ S.Nonempty ∧ |m + ∑ s in S, s| ≤ 1 / (n + 1 : ℝ) := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] ∑ binder: `in` → `∈`\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real Finset\n\n/-- For every finite set X of n real numbers, there exists a non-empty subset S of X \n    and an integer m such that |m + sum of elements in S| ≤ 1/(n+1). -/\ntheorem subset_sum_approximation (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) (X : Finset ℝ) (hX : X.card = n) :\n  ∃ (S : Finset ℝ) (m : ℤ), S ⊆ X ∧ S.Nonempty ∧ |m + ∑ s ∈ S, s| ≤ 1 / (n + 1 : ℝ) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asserts that for every finite set X of n real numbers, there exists a non-empty subset S of X and an integer m such that |m + sum(S)| ≤ 1/(n+1). Key components include: universal quantification over all sets X of size n, existence of non-empty subset S ⊆ X, integer m, and the inequality involving the sum of elements in S.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem parameters (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) (X : Finset ℝ) (hX : X.card = n) correctly capture the universal quantification over all n > 0 and all finite sets X of size n. The conclusion ∃ (S : Finset ℝ) (m : ℤ), S ⊆ X ∧ S.Nonempty ∧ |m + ∑ s ∈ S, s| ≤ 1 / (n + 1 : ℝ) matches the existence claim, subset condition, non-emptiness, and inequality.\n3. Comparative Verification: All components align: Finset ℝ corresponds to the set of real numbers, X.card = n matches the size constraint, S ⊆ X and S.Nonempty capture the subset requirements, and the inequality is correctly formalized with real division. The universal quantification is properly encoded via parameters.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement precisely mirrors the mathematical problem's structure and constraints.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The Lean code correctly formalizes the problem's universal quantification, subset requirements, integer m, and the inequality involving the sum.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real Finset\n\n/-- For every finite set X of n real numbers, there exists a non-empty subset S of X \n    and an integer m such that |m + sum of elements in S| ≤ 1/(n+1). -/\ntheorem subset_sum_approximation (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) (X : Finset ℝ) (hX : X.card = n) :\n  ∃ (S : Finset ℝ) (m : ℤ), S ⊆ X ∧ S.Nonempty ∧ |m + ∑ s ∈ S, s| ≤ 1 / (n + 1 : ℝ) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "/-- Prove that, for every set $X = \\{x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n\\}$ of $n$ real numbers, there exists a non-empty subset $S$ of $X$ and an integer $m$ such that\n\\[\n\\left| m + \\sum_{s \\in S} s \\right| \\leq \\frac{1}{n+1}.\n\\]-/", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 2, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": ["∑ binder: `in` → `∈`"], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "putnam_v4", "question_id": "297"}
